Please read the following sub-decree: Legal Awareness: Disqualification in Civil Cases
(Phnom Penh): In daily life, people are always in contact with each other, and sometimes that relationship also causes conflicts. Some disputes are mediated through out-of-court negotiations to end disputes, but sometimes parties to a dispute choose a judicial mechanism to resolve them.
The judiciary that receives the complaint must deal with the case in accordance with the law, must provide justice to the parties concerned and must maintain its independence, and the individual who is the judge must make decisions on behalf of the Cambodian people, respecting the law in force and the conscience must respect the discretion. Avoid the four biases and maintain absolute bias (impartiality).
In the event that the judge in charge of the case may make a biased and unfair decision, either party to the case may file a motion to disqualify the judge in order to replace the judge in charge of the case. If there is a valid reason according to the law.
To better understand what are the reasons for intending to file a disqualification lawsuit? Therefore, for this week, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "Complaints in Civil Cases" to the following students:
Pursuant to Article 28 of the Code of Civil Procedure, a complaint for disqualification of a judge shall be based on the following reasons: 2. In the event that the parties have argued before the judge or have already made excuses in the pre-trial proceedings before the judge, the parties may not sue the judge.
However, this provision shall not apply to cases in which the parties are unaware of the reasons for filing a disqualification or in cases where the cause of the disqualification lawsuit has arisen at a later date.
In addition to the reasons for the above disqualification, if the party suspects that the judge is biased and cannot give justice to the party, the judge shall be removed from performing his / her duties as defined in Article 27 of the Code of Civil Procedure below. :
2. In such cases as the reasons for the revocation determined in the first paragraph above, the court shall decide on the revocation by application or by its own discretion.
In addition, Article 31 of the Code of Civil Procedure applies equally to registers: The provisions of this section shall apply equally to registers. In this case, the decision on the application and the revocation or disqualification shall be made by the court under the clerk.
That is, even if the clerk does not have the right to decide in case the clerk is close to any party or has had a dispute with any party or has a relationship of interest in the case, because the clerk has a duty to And plays an important role in ensuring impartiality in case management.
In summary, if the parties to a dispute find that their judge or clerk is unfairly biased, the parties may file a complaint against the judge or clerk in charge of the case in the court where the judge is located.
Once the dismissal proceedings have been processed, the grievance proceedings shall be adjourned pending the outcome of the Trial Chamber's decision on the dismissal proceedings by a judge or clerk.
