Cambodia asks Thailand to allow workers to return to work in Thailand and help reduce the cost of chariots
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has requested the Royal Government of Thailand to allow Cambodian workers holding border cards and registered workers under the Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) to be able to return to work in Thailand and help reduce the cost of working for 14 days. Well.
The request was made on October 2, 2020, on the occasion of Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia, having a video conference with Deputy Prime Minister Don Pramat Vinay, Minister of Foreign Affairs of Thailand.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs stated that Mr. Don Pramatvinai praised the Royal Government of Cambodia for its great success in preventing and controlling the spread of Kovid-19 throughout the country. He also thanked the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs for facilitating the repatriation of Thai citizens during Kovid-19.
According to the statement, the two Deputy Prime Ministers stressed the importance of information exchange and close cooperation to fight Kovid-19, and hoped that Thailand's vaccine would be successfully developed and launched. Coming soon.
Both sides enjoy strong bilateral trade despite the spread of Kovid-19, with Cambodia requesting the Thai side to facilitate cross-border shipments, increase Cambodia's agricultural imports and expedite the signing of a memorandum of understanding. Understand cross-border transportation and other important work.
Both sides expressed great satisfaction with the opening of new regional and international border crossings and the upgrading of existing regional border crossings to international border crossings. Cambodia is pleased to note that Thai investment figures in Cambodia have nearly tripled in the first eight months of 2020 compared to last year.
Cambodia encourages Thai investors to invest more in Cambodia. Both sides also expressed satisfaction and noted the excellent progress in other key areas including security, education, tourism, energy and connectivity.
The two sides stressed the importance of better implementation of joint statements and bilateral agreements between the two countries and expected the Fourth Joint Council of Ministers and the Joint Committee on Bilateral Cooperation. The 11th party will be convened at the most appropriate time after the situation of Kovid-19 has improved.
Both sides are firmly committed to supporting each other in multilateral frameworks such as ASEAN and the Asia-Europe Summit. The two heads of diplomacy also expressed their appreciation for the remarkable achievements made so far on the occasion of the 70th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Cambodia and Thailand.
The two Deputy Prime Ministers reaffirmed their strong commitment to promoting long-term friendship, good neighborliness, cooperation in all fields and partnership for peace and prosperity between the two Kingdoms.
