Nearly 1,000 luxury saplings are planted by the Ministry of Environment and the city administration on public roads: pagodas and schools to create greenery to cultivate people's love for the environment
(Tbong Khmum): Nearly 1,000 luxury saplings, including 600 rosewood saplings, 100 saplings and 150 yellow flower peacock saplings are planted along National Road 7 at pagodas, schools and public roads. In the morning of October 3, 2020, under the chairmanship of Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, with the participation of more than 200 government officials, monks, students and people.
This is an activity that is valued as raising awareness of the environment and natural resources and cultivating the spirit of people to love and respect the environment, and together plant more trees and participate in the protection and conservation of natural resources, forests and wildlife.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment stated that every year, hundreds of thousands of rosewood trees, including beng, Neang, Nuon, Thnong, are planted by local authorities in various parts of Cambodia, both on public roads, in schools, pagodas and in local areas. Degraded forests.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra called the vigorous activities of tree planting throughout the country as a movement to inspire the spirit of loving the environment and natural resources and reforestation and to participate in the protection and conservation for the benefit of generations. Going forward.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment added that the tree that each of us planted today means that Cambodia will have one more tree on the existing forest resources in our existing protected area. Must participate in protection and conservation.
The senior environmental official appealed to people across the country: "Let the Cambodian people plant trees together to restore lost forest cover and protect the remaining natural resources of Cambodia's forests." .
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, Cambodia has been striving to protect its natural resources by establishing several protected areas and conservation areas, which so far have 66 protected areas and More than 7.3 million hectares, equivalent to more than 41% of Cambodia's total land area, are under the management and conservation of the Ministry of Environment.
