The Ministry of Environment led journalists on a three-day visit to explore the implementation of the REDD + Seima project in Mondulkiri province
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment led a group of multi-institutional journalists to visit the Red Bok Seima project in Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri province in the northeastern region of Cambodia from 3 to 5 days. October 2020.
The visit was led by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, and was also attended by a delegation from the Ministry of Environment and some stakeholders.
The study tour was co-organized by WCS and the Ministry of the Environment, supported by USAID, with the aim of providing an opportunity for delegates and journalists to explore and raise public awareness of: Implement the Keo Seima Red Plus project, protection and conservation of biodiversity resources and programs to improve the livelihoods of local communities.
During the visit, the delegation will meet with representatives of Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment, Director of Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary, rangers and representatives of WCS at the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary to discuss the implementation of the REDD + project and related mechanisms. Distribute benefits to the community.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra thanked all relevant partner organizations, especially USAID, which continued to contribute to the protection and conservation of the Kingdom of Cambodia's forests to survive, leading to the sale of carbon credits in the sanctuary. Prey Keo Seima.
He claimed that the contributions of all partners not only benefit the ecosystem, but also benefit the local community through the creation of eco-tourism sites.
He thanked for the good cooperation between the Ministry of Environment and all partners and thanked the rangers, local authorities at all levels and the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment for their high commitment to the conservation of biodiversity and wildlife. In Cambodia for the next generation of Cambodia.
REDD + is a mechanism designed to encourage developing countries to participate in protecting, managing and using the forest resources in their countries better and smarter to contribute to the fight against global climate change. Mr.
The Keo Seima Red Plus project was launched in 2010 and focuses on the core area (166,983 hectares) within the protected area. The project covers 20 target villages with a total population of about 18,000 people who benefit from better protected area management and support for community development.
The Ministry of Environment, a partner of WCS, has sold nearly $ 4 million in carbon credits to date. Proceeds from the sale of these carbon credits are now being used to fully support forest conservation and management activities, as well as to support forest and wildlife management skills training for government officials.
It should be noted that Mondulkiri Province, located in the northeastern region of the Kingdom of Cambodia, was established in 1961 by His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, His Majesty the King Father, Father of Independence, Territorial Integrity and Khmer National Unity. 14,288 square kilometers of land ranks first among the 25 provincial capitals. Divided into 4 districts and a city: Koh Nhek, Chbar, Oreang, Pichreda and Sen Monorom.
Mondulkiri Province is bordered on the north by Stung Treng and Ratanakkiri Provinces, on the east and south by the Socialist Republic of Vietnam and on the west by Kratie Province. Mondulkiri is 380 km from Phnom Penh on National Roads 6, 8 and 7 through Kandal, Prey Veng, Tbong Khmum and Kratie provinces.
A child contracted severe rabies due to a dog bite and was not treated early
(Banteay Meanchey): A child was infected with severe rabies due to a dog bite and was not treated in time. The child was bitten by a dog on September 11, 2020 and was hospitalized on the morning of October 2, 2020 at the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Provincial Hospital, Mongkul Borei District, Banteay Meanchey Province.
Dr. Le Chansangvat, director of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Health Department, said that the children who were bitten by dogs were named Kuoy Reak, father Mao Khemara and mother Sok Savann, living in Santepheap village, Tuol Pong commune, Malai district, Banteay Meanchey province.
The boy was bitten by a dog about 20 days ago and was taken home from the Japanese Friendship Hospital in Mongkol Borei. According to the doctor, there are severe symptoms of rabies.
Dr. Le Chansangvat further confirmed that the child was decided to be sent to the Battambang Provincial Hospital for treatment, but the child who was bitten by a dog may not be able to be cured because the dog venom has reached a serious level. .
