Senior Social Affairs official: The government is working to minimize human trafficking and exploitation
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has been working to develop, compile, disseminate and implement policies, strategies, plans, bilateral and multilateral agreements, and mechanisms aimed at combating and minimizing human trafficking and exploitation. On people within the national, sub-regional, regional and global.
Human trafficking has been rampant in the form of transnational and organized crime, and it has had a negative impact on individuals, families and communities, especially at the national, regional and global levels.
This was stated by Mr. Nhek Vannara, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, Representative of Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation By human trafficking "on October 1, 2020 at the Cambodiana Hotel, Phnom Penh.
He said that the Ministry of Social Affairs has actively worked and achieved many achievements in protecting victims of human trafficking, policy-making, prevention of human trafficking, strengthening cooperation and enhancing partnerships. Despite the Ministry's many achievements, the protection of victims of trafficking has not yet been fully addressed, especially the provision of support services for victims.
Mr. Nhek Vannara hopes that the parties present today will make every effort to discuss and provide valuable feedback to this workshop so that the Technical Working Group can revise the minimum defense policies and standards. The rights of victims of human trafficking to be more effective and to serve the best interests of our victims.
(Kampong Chhnang): Kampong Chhnang provincial authorities have built another new market in Chhnuk Trou village, Chhnuk Trou commune, Baribo district to alleviate the hardships of people who used to move their stalls along the river up and down every year. This new market has beautiful and safe shops to serve the people selling goods.
Chhnuk Trou commune chief confirmed that so far, Chhnuk Trou fresh sea market has been completed with 224 shops. People who can afford to come and rent a shop in the Central Market by depositing $ 1,500 per place for a three-year sale.
In particular, those who can not afford to pay rent, which affects the livelihood of Kovid-19 disease, can pay in installments according to the actual possibility during the closing of the sale before the deadline of 3 years, must notify the market commission three months in advance for the deposit is Removable. The monthly stall fee is $ 35 per stall.
