Labor Minister submits three proposals to the Japanese Embassy for the benefit of the nation and the people of Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, made three proposals in front of the Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia, Mr. Mikami Masahiro, to bring benefits to the nation and the people of Cambodia. The three proposals include further support for human resource development, encouraging Japanese investors to increase investment in Cambodia and expand the market for Cambodian industrial products in Japan.
The Minister's request was made during a meeting with Ambassador Mikami Masahiro at the Ministry of Labor on October 1, 2020.
During the meeting, both sides praised the good cooperation between Cambodia and Japan in all fields. In particular, the field of employment and vocational training is also a framework for cooperation in which both sides continue to promote and expand new cooperation. The governments of both countries have put in place effective measures to prevent the spread of Kovid-19.
The Japanese Ambassador commended the Royal Government of Cambodia for its effective measures on the Kovid-19 issue and for measures to address the effects of the epidemic, as it has helped address both social and economic issues.
Within the framework of Cambodia-Japan labor cooperation, a memorandum of understanding was signed in 2007 to receive Cambodian interns to work in Japan and a memorandum of understanding in 2019 to accept skilled workers to work in Japan. There are more than 10,000 Cambodian interns and skilled workers working in Japan.
The Minister also thanked the Japanese side for taking care of the Cambodian trainees and skilled workers, as well as allowing them to enter and leave Japan through home remodeling measures. As planned, Cambodia will send more trained trainees and skilled workers to Japan as soon as both sides agree on the means and procedures to ensure the transmission of Kovid-19 virus.
In promoting human resource development, the Minister stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia is promoting the implementation of education, technical and vocational education and training policies in response to the development of industry and ensuring Cambodia's competitiveness. The policy is also in need of assistance from other partners, and Japan's JICA is also assisting with electrical skills training.
Minister Ith Sam Heng informed that the Ministry has recently requested JICA to have more electrical technicians to help teach and hope that Japan will agree to this proposal.
Mr. Mikami, as well as Mr. Ith Sam Heng, all expressed the view that Japanese investors have really contributed significantly to the development of the Cambodian economy to grow continuously. Japanese-invested industries have not closed their production lines, but have continued to increase their investments, such as Minibear, one of Japan's leading manufacturers of small motor components.
Both sides said that harmonizing professional relations is an indispensable element for investors. So far, good relations between Cambodian workers and Japanese employers have been close, understanding and tolerant, which is an important factor in attracting more Japanese investors to Cambodia.
