Senators and senior officials of the Ministry of Interior urge Koh Kong officials to study technology to easily transmit information to higher officials
(Koh Kong): Mr. Kong Sarach, Chairman of the Regional Committee and Chairman of the Ninth Committee of the Senate, and Mr. Prak Sam Oeun, Director General of the General Department of Administration, Ministry of Interior, urged provincial administrative officers, commune clerks and local officials in Koh Kong province to study Proficient in technology and digital systems, easy to transmit information and quickly to higher officials from local issues.
Mr. Kong Sarach added that all countries in the world are already working together to this point. Therefore, Cambodia must strive to make this digital system more widely available for human resources to know how to use it properly in order to contribute to the growth of the society and achieve high middle income in 2030.
He added that commune councils throughout Koh Kong province need to be aware of the progress and serve local development, especially to be able to easily communicate with people to address their concerns. Fast, and so on.
"Once the commune chiefs have the ability to use digital communication, they can send information about what the people want to their leaders in a timely manner," he added. Seek solutions to challenges and raise common development goals effectively. "Although not 100 percent good, the authorities can avoid any response."
During the forum, Mr. Prak Sam Oeun also noted that during the Kovid-19 epidemic crisis, some professional and administrative officials were able to use digital technology better. He also encouraged all relevant officials in Koh Kong province to continue their efforts for the socio-economic development of the country through industrial reform, technology and democracy.
Koh Kong Provincial Governor Mithona Phuthong also agreed with Kong Sarach and Prak Sam Oeun. As a provincial official, Ms. Mithona Phu Thong affirmed that she will continue to explore other ways to mobilize government officials and the public to use the electronic system for everyone as a basis to consider and address any challenges that arise. Basic.
