Please be informed that students who are going to go to study for a master's degree at various universities in Japan next month and Australia with New Zealand in early January 2021.
The scholarship program aims to contribute to the development of human resources in Cambodia by providing scholarships for training in all areas, especially key sectors such as economics, trade, land management, public services, technology, education and agriculture to contribute to the development of the country. Cambodia, in line with their respective skills and potential.
Banteay Meanchey Provincial Governor Visits and Provides Drinks to Intervention Forces
(Banteay Meanchey): On the afternoon of September 28, 2020, the Governor of Banteay Meanchey Province, Mr. Oum Rath, visited the 51st Infantry Brigade and the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Military Operation Force, which was helping to harvest the flooded rice of the people. 20 hectares in Kramol village, Rohat Teuk commune, Mongkul Borei district, and also brought 20 cans of orange juice, 10 cans of M150 kacao water and 300 liters of gasoline to the forces that are intervening to help the people.
During the visit, Mr. Oum Rath praised the strength of the forces of the two units who devoted their time and energy to harvest the rice that was flooded.
He said that without timely assistance, the flooded rice fields would be completely damaged, although it could not be harvested completely, but some could be recovered from the floods to provide food for a while.
Ms. Kong Sopheap, Rohat Teuk commune chief, said that the people in this commune have been cultivating a lot of rice this year. Prolonged drought at the beginning of the season causes the rice that you made to die almost completely. The few remaining rice fields were inundated by heavy rains after the rice was almost ripe for harvest.
Colonel Sing Tum, commander of the 51st Infantry Brigade, said that this was the second day that the brigade had come to help harvest the farmers in this village. Although it was a bit difficult to soak in the hot water for a long time in the face of the onslaught of the beasts, the brothers and sisters were happy to help until the end.
At the same time, Mr. Oum Rath called on people with small children to be extremely careful not to play in the water to climb trees to avoid drowning or biting.
