(Kep): On September 27, 2020, the Ministry of Tourism organized a consultative workshop on the preliminary draft of Kep Tourism Development Master Plan 2020-2035 under the chairmanship of Mr. Tith Chantha, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Tourism on behalf of the Minister. Thong Khon and Mr. Som Piseth, Governor of Kep Province.
The workshop was held at Kep Provincial Hall with the participation of representatives of relevant ministries and institutions in the inter-ministerial committee to prepare the master plan for tourism development in Kep and Mondulkiri provinces, as well as other relevant government officials and private sector.
On that occasion, the workshop discussed and evaluated the situation, identified challenges and put in some input in the development of tourism development strategies and related sectors that support tourism development in Kep to become a tourist destination in this province. Become a high-end luxury resort as indicated by the Royal Government of Cambodia.
The workshop also supported the identification and establishment of four priority poles for tourism development in Kep, including:
First, the Old Kep Tourism Pole is the main tourist pole of the province, which is rich in potential, coastal areas, mangrove forests, mountainous national parks and heritage areas.
Second: Angkol tourism pole is a new development area with potential for mangrove beaches and attractive mountainous areas.
Third, the Phnom Vally tourism pole is a natural, agro-tourism and historical tourist site rich in pepper plantations, durian and other long-term fruit crops.
Fourth, the tourism pole of the archipelago is an area with potential for underwater resources such as corals, fish, seagrass, which is a source of food for marine life, sea dolphins, pigs and other fish.
The workshop also stated that the preparation of the master plan for the development of tourism in Kep is to ensure the development of tourism in Kep is different from the other three coastal provinces of Cambodia (Sihanoukville, Kampot and Koh Kong). Based on the principle of "increasing competitiveness, creating a vibrant identity and features, innovation and responsibility."
It should be informed that the Royal Government has established an inter-ministerial commission led by the Ministry of Tourism to prepare a master plan for tourism development in three provinces (in Cambodia's three major tourism poles), including: Siem Reap Tourism Development Master Plan, Mondul Province Kiri and Kep provinces. The "Art of Our Country" scene in September is more unique and amazing than before
