(Phnom Penh): In the daily work process, all employers and workers working in the Kingdom of Cambodia must strictly comply with the provisions of the Labor Law 1997 and related proclamations.
With regard to leave, employees have the right to take leave as permitted by law, but some employers have violated by banning workers from taking leave by threatening to cut wages or using excuses to quit their jobs.
In the past, many workers have been subjected to this oppression because they were unaware of their right to leave and the right to be paid for some leave.
This week, Fresh News' Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, brings the topic of "paid leave" to citizens, students, and especially workers. Specify what types of leave that employees can still be paid for, and what are the rights and conditions that must be met?
For the benefit of workers, the Labor Law of the Kingdom of Cambodia requires employers to pay workers for six types of leave, including weekly leave, holiday leave, sick leave, maternity leave, and leave. Annual and special leave.
3. Termination of illness is the right to give employees the right to request sick leave when there is no pay cut. In case of stopping for more than 2 days, the employee must have a valid doctor's certificate attached to the application. Sick leave does not count as annual leave (Article 166, paragraph 5 of the Labor Law).
5. Annual paid leave refers to paid leave on a normal working day, which is not a national holiday, without deductions. Generally, workers have the right to one and a half days off per month or 18 days per year based on Article 166 of the Labor Law.
It should also be noted that this annual leave with pay can only be applied if the employee has completed one full year of work. The duration of this leave shall be extended according to the seniority of the employees in the enterprise, with one day extended for a period of three years.
In summary, all employers who own establishments or enterprises should adhere to the above paid holiday principles and avoid violating workers' rights as illegal activities. . At the same time, workers must be aware of their rights and benefits and use them within the framework set by law.
