However, sometimes Cambodia has become a victim or hostage because of its politically hostile enemies. This is because the superpowers who see Cambodia as a beautiful bride quarrel over Cambodia even though this beautiful Cambodian bride does not know anything.
Sometimes a superpower envies Cambodia just by seeing this tiny country have a good relationship with another superpower called "false jealousy." In real life, there are many men or women who suffer from jealousy.
After all, the United States, which is gaining geopolitical influence with China, sometimes envies Cambodia with China when it sees Cambodia have good relations with this Asian giant. False jealousy is the reason why this Western world power started looking for China by imposing sanctions on a Chinese company that is investing in Cambodia.
Although the United States has cited human rights issues as an excuse, everyone knows that "is just an excuse to oppress China in terms of geopolitical conquest."
In a situation where Cambodia is under pressure from rival powers, it is crucial that all Cambodians of all political persuasions strive to protect their common interests. Cambodians may have different political views, but Cambodians still have one common interest: the national interest as a whole. Therefore, when common interests are threatened, Cambodians must unite to protect their interests, leaving political interests aside.
We, the Cambodian people, must understand that our country is in a geographical location that is vulnerable to the influence of superpowers. Even if we take a smart political diplomacy, it is difficult to completely avoid the vapors of conflict between superpowers. It is not different from a hurricane that occurs in one country, but its steam affects other countries, especially neighboring countries.
The situation that Cambodia is currently facing is the so-called "suffering because politics has no enemies." However, it is better than suffering because politics builds enemies, as some Cambodian politicians have been doing. No-enemy politics can allow Cambodia to join hands with all countries with a smile. This policy can also allow Cambodia to quickly compensate for the damage.
On the contrary, the politics of building an enemy by standing with one great power and declaring an enemy with the other great power will surely cause Cambodia to suffer as it did in the past. Let Cambodians remember this point.
September 25, 2020 marks the 51st anniversary of the death of Khmer literary scholar Chuon Nath
(Phnom Penh): Friday, September 25, 2020 is the 51st anniversary of the death of Samdech Preah Sangkareach Chuon Nath, Choat Gnhano, a member of the Mahanikaya of the Kingdom of Cambodia, a famous Khmer literary scholar. He created many works, especially the Khmer dictionary for the next generation of Khmer children.
According to a document released by the National Language Institute of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, it was confirmed that Samdech Preah Sangkhareach Chuon Nath was born on Tuesday, March 11, 1883. Died at the age of 86 at 8:20 pm at the Royal House of Wat Ounalom, Phnom Penh.
Samdech was born into a peasant family, his mother was named Samdech, and his father was named Brahma Chuon. The family has only two children, Prince Samdech Preah Sangkhareach and Mr. Oknha Sophon Mantry Chuon Nuth, the younger brother of Samdech Preah Sangkhareach. When he was about 12 years old, his parents brought him to study literature with a right-wing teacher named Ke Mam at Wat Po Pruek, also known as Wat (Prayum), Sangkat Roleang Ken, Kandal Stung District, Kandal Province.
As a child, he studied national literature until he was 18 years old. He stayed there for two years before studying at Wat Ounalom, Phnom Penh, the temple where he also died.
At the age of 21, he completed his training as a monk and lived in Wat Por Prek, Kandal Stung District, Kandal Province in 2448 AD 1904. At that time, he received the name of the commandment of Chothanano. In the field of education, he first began to study silk and the translation of the Dharma and the Dharma from the preacher Market, the Upachaya.
Prince Chuon Nath learned in addition to the national language, he also learned Sanskrit Pali grammar, which at that time, between 1910, among Pali scholars, he was known as a Thera. Proficiency and proficiency in the Pali language. He also learned many other languages, including Thai, Lao, French, English and Vietnamese.
Samdech Preah Sangkhareach has been energetic in his study of both Pali and other languages, including French, and his studies have been very successful. His Majesty, if he starts to study any subject, he pays attention and works very hard, there is no reduction in the study of that subject, such as Pali.
Samdech Preah Sangkhareach Chuon Nath received that education, he studied with a strong will, not studying any subject by play. Pali, Sanskrit, and French are all subjects with the most thorough and profound grammar, difficult to learn, extremely difficult to understand, but Samdech Preah Sangkhareach can learn to understand clearly very quickly, which is very admirable.
He knew and understood the grammar of all the languages that he had learned, such as Pali, Sanskrit, both the national language and the words of ancient culture, and he could remember many reasons. The Lao, Thai and French languages that Samdech learned were able to communicate with the indigenous people. Samdech Chhlat Wei Prasop imitated the sounds of other national languages in a similar or similar way to English, Vietnamese, which Samdech learned to be able to see, write and communicate.
In 1913, Samdech entered the sentence contest in Preah Uposat Ratanaram, Vihear Preah Keo Morakot in the face of His Majesty King Samdech Preah Sisowath, four sentences, which is the highest sentence in that period. In 1915, Samdech took the second examination and was appointed a professor at the Senior Pali School, now known as Suramarit Buddhist High School.
