Conservationists are thrilled as they look at rare images of three adult female reindeer and one juvenile reindeer living in a rich wildlife sanctuary out of a total of 1,710 photographs. Taken between August and September by deploying automatic cameras at various locations in the sanctuary, covering a total area of 50,093 hectares.
"We are all very excited to record the presence of these reindeer," said Eam Sam On, WWF Mekong Regional Biodiversity Survey and Monitoring Officer. .
Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF-Cambodia, also said: “These rare images of reindeer are a testament to the effectiveness and efficiency of our protected area management and conservation work, which brings hope to Protect this species both in Cambodia and in the region.
Mr. Seng Teak said that in addition to reindeer, the automatic camera also captures images of wild boar, vultures, vultures, fragrant vultures, as well as some other important large species of birds such as green peacocks and small watermelons.
A report on endangered species released by the IUCN in 2015 shows that the number of reindeer is scattered around the world, and it is estimated that there are less than 700 of them. Only a handful remain in the protected forest areas of Cambodia.
WWF has been working closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia at all levels to support the management of rich wildlife sanctuaries, where habitat loss and hunting for meat and for trade are major threats. On natural resources and wildlife survival.
"Reindeer are classified as endangered species in both Cambodia's forest law and the IUCN Red List," the spokesman said. On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the Ministry of Environment would like to thank all stakeholders involved in the protection and conservation of wildlife in Cambodia.
The Ministry of Environment calls on all citizens to participate in the protection and conservation of these reindeer living in Cambodia's protected areas for generations to come.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized that the Ministry of Environment recognizes that despite efforts to prevent and crack down on crime day and night, small-scale natural resource crimes still occur, the Ministry of Environment will continue to cooperate with relevant authorities. Suppress and prevent further.
Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary is located in Sambo District, Kratie Province, and another protected area called the Junction Wildlife Sanctuary has been officially declared a "Wildlife Sanctuary Protected Area" by a Royal Government sub-decree. Both sanctuaries support untouched natural forest habitats and major river systems while their population density is low.
