Minister Veng Sakhon: Changing Roles and Duties is a Necessary Condition for Civil Servants
(Phnom Penh): During the inauguration ceremony of civil servants at the Ministry of Agriculture this morning, September 23, 2020, Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, stated that a change of roles and responsibilities is a necessary condition. Within the framework of civil servants to improve the quality and efficiency of leadership and management in an institution.
The ceremony was also attended by a large number of Ministry leaders, ladies and gentlemen, civil servants under the Ministry.
Minister Veng Sakhon, on behalf of the Ministry and on his own behalf, expressed his deep gratitude to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, for considering and agreeing to the Ministry's request to appoint the Ministry's leadership. And some civil servants in order to participate in the development of the agricultural sector by improving the quality and efficiency of leadership and management of work in each professional unit to be better.
In order to achieve the policy of agricultural development (2019-2023) and especially to achieve the goals and achieve the ambitions of the Royal Government, Minister Veng Sakhon highlighted the important points to inform the whole ceremony and the new officials. The new position is used as a key to lead, manage, serve and solve problems that may occur both at the local level and at their own units, which are summarized below:
First, agricultural officials must go to the grassroots to implement the recommendations of Samdech Techo to serve and solve the problems of farmers in a comprehensive, timely and effective manner under all circumstances and events that may occur in the crisis of Kovid-19.
Second, continue to transfer modern technology to promote fish, fish and crops suitable for climate change, environment, sustainability and competition to improve production capacity, ensure quantity, quality and safety. Establish mechanisms to provide information on agricultural markets, strengthen research, develop technology, plan planting, harvesting and processing, and continue to strengthen sanitation and phytosanitary capacity building.
Thirdly, the Capital-Provincial Departments of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and other subordinate units must have a network to receive information and suggestions from the community, civil society and all partners to review and resolve in a timely manner.
Seventh, to update all the objectives of the projects to suit the actual situation and urgent needs of local farmers in the circumstances of the Kovid-19 epidemic crisis. Review the principles of import, export and transit of all kinds of animals, animal products, fish and agricultural inputs to avoid the impact or burden on domestic production.
