Environmental activists protest against oil exploration in Norway, 40 arrested
(Oslo): Norway is under increasing pressure as environmental activists continue to protest against a halt to oil and gas exploration in Norwegian waters. This is according to the press release from Sputnik on Tuesday, September 22, 2020.
In another demonstration, activists gathered outside the Norwegian Ministry of Commerce and Fisheries, dressed in fishermen's clothing and holding hands on walls to demand that the government recognize global warming and the destruction of biogas systems. It has been argued that the oil industry will not be able to provide a future for the Norwegian people.
Two trucks with the same fake license plate from Battambang province passing through Kampong Chhnang province were intercepted by the police
(Kampong Chhnang): After inspecting the irregularities of two trucks with the same license plate, the goods were transported from Battambang province through Kampong Chhnang province by the prosecutor of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Court and ordered the police Economy of the Provincial Police to check the license plates and goods on the car.
The two trucks were semi-trailers after the police detained them at the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Police Traffic Office and found that they were using license plates. The same fakes, of course.
Both of these vehicles use fake license plates, which is completely against the law, which can be charged with using fake public documents. Now, for the two vehicles, after moving the goods out, he will take them to the land traffic office to wait for the court to take action.
According to unofficial sources, on the day the police stopped the two vehicles, all three had the same license plate, while the other went missing. Two vehicles were detained by the authorities, and when all the above vehicles were unloaded at the Ly Kea Dry Port in Battambang, all the vehicles changed their license plates to Takeo 3A-1438 before the customs inspected and all the vehicles as well. It is set every 2 hours.
The source further revealed that all trucks passing through the province did not pass inspection in each province as far as Phnom Penh. And all of the above vehicles, when the authorities immediately stopped to ask for permission to transport, found no documents related to the transport.
The Prime Minister of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Samdech Techo, made this statement at a high-level meeting commemorating the 75th anniversary of the United Nations on September 21, 2020. The summit was held under the theme: "The Future We All Want, the United Nations We Need: Reaffirm Joint Efforts for Multilateralism."
In his keynote address at this important UN forum, Samdech Techo Hun Sen recalled the preamble to the post-World War II UN Constitution, which focuses on the use of "international mechanisms." For economic and social development for all people. This mechanism is an essential means of ensuring peace, promoting human rights and ensuring social progress.
For Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the mechanisms and perspectives of the United Nations are being undermined and need immediate attention. In fact, the multilateral mechanism is under heavy attack due to the extreme nationalism and protectionism of some of the world's great powers.
Samdech Techo, Head of the Royal Government, emphasized that the competition between the great powers has hindered the right to self-determination of developing countries in choosing independence and sovereignty as a path for development, as well as in choosing friendly partners for peace and prosperity. Development.
"I think this is a major obstacle for developing countries to achieve the 2030 Agenda in addition to the immediate challenges posed by the epidemic," he said. "Kovid-19 and its social and economic impacts."
In this keynote address, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also highlighted the agenda of respect for human rights in developing countries, which is often used by developed countries as a target for sanctions on developing countries.
"Developing countries are often the target of sanctions for their imperfect nation-building efforts, which are not in line with the standards of developed countries," he said. "There is a lack of protection for basic human rights, such as the right to peace, the right to life, the right to food, shelter and employment."
According to Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, this problem is one of the reasons that contributes to the serious destruction of the efforts to restore and develop the nation of poor countries. In particular, through the modernization of human rights issues serve the geopolitical agenda of some developed countries.
In this speech, Samdech Techo expressed his belief that member states must abide by the provisions of the Declaration on the 75th Anniversary of the United Nations adopted at this meeting. According to Samdech Techo Prime Minister, the success of the ambitious goals set by all member states will pave the way for the next generation to be able to live in prosperity and in a society with an environment and harmony.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also stated that Cambodia recognizes the central role of the multilateral system in addressing the complex global challenges facing today, as stated in the declaration: "Our challenges are significant. "Interlocking and can only be solved through a multilateral system that needs to be improved."
He stressed that by respecting the UN Charter, transparency, solidarity, cooperation and inclusiveness, the international community will be able to achieve the future we all want. Yes, and a United Nations that we need.
