(World): Flying is a popular way for people to travel long distances across continents, cross-country, etc., but some questions have been raised as to why this iron-winged bird needs to fly. Above the cloud surface? And why is it so loud when boarding and landing?
At take-off and landing, the aircraft is exposed to the dense atmosphere closest to the Earth, so it is necessary to use a lot of energy to get out of the clouds or down to the ground to get its engine installed. Loud sound and ringing force.
Generally, aircraft have to fly at a maximum altitude of about 11 kilometers from the ground. Flying at such altitudes is due to three main reasons: saving fuel, speeding up and avoiding bad obstacles.
When the aircraft is flying at high altitudes above the clouds, it does not need to use a lot of engine power, which means that when it flies at an altitude of more than 10 km above the clouds, there will be no obstacles to disturb the aircraft. Try to increase energy and spend more fuel.
In the area above the cloud surface, there is not much wind, which is very good for the plane to fly at high speed. On average, it can fly at 1,000 kilometers per hour.
Another major reason is the avoidance of natural disasters such as hurricanes, thick clouds, high mountains, and large birds. At this point, you probably already know that most planes are dangerous during take-off and landing.
