General Knowledge: Before World War II, Japan was the only Asian power with ambitions to swallow the world.
(Japan): The army of emperors in the Japanese colonial era had great power in Asia to inspire the ambition of this nation to dare to swallow the world. What does Japan plan to do to achieve its ambitions? And what are the obstacles that make Japan fail to swallow this world?
* History and leaders of ancient Japan
Japan was then ruled by Emperor Showa from 1926-1945, before Japan lost World War II, but after World War II Withdrawal of power from Japan during the GHQ / SCAP period from 1945-1989. After the transition period, Japan was replaced by a constitutional monarchy from 1989 to the present.
* Modern Japan and early Western relations
At a time when Europe was booming, only the Dutch were the first to arrive in Japan with the right to trade in the port of Nagasaki. The United States then forced Japan to open its doors to foreign influence until 1854, when the United States engaged in trade activities at several Japanese ports.
In a short period of time, Japan became one of the fastest growing superpowers in Europe, during which time it was nicknamed the "Emerald Light Era", which means a prosperous period.
* The Emerald Age led the Japanese army to conquer the colony.
During the reign of Emperor Mujihito, Japan pursued a colonial policy similar to that of the European powers, opening the first colony on the Qing Dynasty of China in 1894 to occupy Korea, which was then under occupation. Chinese domination. Victory over Chinese troops led Japan to recapture Taiwan from the Great Wall.
Along with the growth of the military, the industrial colonies of Japan also experienced some major crises: the lack of agricultural products to meet the growing needs of its people and the lack of raw materials for industrial and manufacturing processes. Weapons are needed in foreign battles. The lack of a large market for products is also a serious problem for Japan.
Japan had ambitions to swallow the whole of Asia, but lost the war with the United States, especially in the case of the atomic bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945. US troops have risen to the Pacific, squeezing Japan almost to the brim and putting it under transition to break the Asian military power.
However, the new Japan under the constitutional monarchy sought to rebuild the nation to become the world's second largest economy after the United States, which later became the world's second largest economy. 3 back.
Despite being a world economic power, Japan is considered a political and military power because the US-written constitution restricts Japan to a small number of self-defense forces. Established in 1954. Court issues arrest warrant for gunman killed in car crash
