Ms. Ly Ilan: Cambodia must continue to focus on the fight against Kovid-19
In just six months, the number of Kovid-19 positive cases worldwide has reached 10 million, and in just one and a half months, the number of Kovid-19 positive cases has doubled to 20 million. As of September 15, more than 29 million positive cases of Kovid-19 virus, including more than 920,000 deaths, had been reported to the World Health Organization.
Around the world, some countries have had a series of Kovid-19 outbreaks that have been going on for months, while others have kept the infection at a low level, but there has been a resurgence of the disease. Kovid-19, including some countries in the western Pacific, which covers Cambodia.
That is why we cannot be indifferent and careless. Indifference is dangerous.
However, many of the people I interviewed told me about their concerns that many citizens were pretending to be free of the Kovid-19 virus at all. Public indifference to health measures is a major issue. Effective implementation of national policies and guidelines at the local level remains a challenge. Local authorities and health workers know that Kovid-19 can return at any time.
"Today we have entered a new phase of the Kovid-19 epidemic in the Asia-Pacific region," said the World Health Organization (WHO) Regional Director for the Western Pacific. At this stage, as long as the virus continues to spread and as long as the population does not have an immune system, we must live in a new way that can protect public health and allow society and the economy to recover and return to normal. Some normal.
The Kovid-19 outbreak is not the first outbreak and will not be the last. Every crisis is always an opportunity for change for a better future. The government, the private sector, the religious community and individuals play an important role in the fight against Kovid-19 and in building a better and safer future.
During festivals such as Pchum Ben, it is important that those in charge of places, ceremonies, and cultural events ensure that safety gaps, hand hygiene, and masks are properly followed. . We must all work together to reduce the risk of transmission of Kovid-19 virus and prevent its transmission to the community.
