The history of Ansam cakes and the beliefs of the Khmer people!
(Phnom Penh): Ansam cake is a cake that almost all Cambodian families always prepare Ansam cake every year during the Khmer New Year, especially Pchum Ben season, in addition to these two festivals in the wedding ceremony. They are also often seen baking cakes. It is often noticed that one or two days before Pchum Ben or Pchum Thom, Cambodians often hurry to prepare Ansam cakes to participate in Pchum Ben.
"Ansam Cake" has its origins in ancient Khmer history and has been seen in traditional Khmer festivals such as New Year, Pchum Ben, weddings and other religious ceremonies. Bringing all these cakes to the pagoda on Pchum Ben day comes from this festival as well as other festivals related to Hinduism.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit are leaders who adhere to the Khmer religion. On the occasion of Kan Ben for the past 14 days, although they did not go to the temple directly, Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit also arranged food and alms to participate in Kan Ben at the temples. .
Separately, for Wat Veang Chas, Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, as well as their families, always go to Pchum or Pchum Thom almost every year.
In addition to dedicating the relics to the ancestors, through the merits of the merits born of this generosity, Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit also dedicate the souls of the Khmer ancestors, warriors, warriors and patriots who sacrificed and sacrificed their lives. In the social cause as well.
It should be noted that since the Old Palace Pagoda is an old monastery site associated with the history of Cambodia, the former Royal Palace of the King of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit have led the rebuilding of this pagoda since 1996. The rebuilding of this pagoda was dedicated to Buddhism and, in turn, to preserve historical and cultural sites for future generations to study.
Wat Veang Chas was originally called Wat Harirak Reachini. Later, this pagoda was called Wat Nai Veang or Wat Veang Chas respectively. In the early days, this pagoda was prosperous as a pagoda rich in Pali and Samrai scriptures. It is a pagoda that cultivates a large number of monks as doctors under the patronage of the Khmer King. But the artifacts and achievements of the temple have been destroyed by war and genocide.
After January 7, 1979, this temple, as well as other monasteries, reopened. But at first there was only one salum built on the hill of the old church. Until 1996, Samdech Techo Prime Minister and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, an active Buddhist supporter, led the rebuilding of this historic temple and made significant progress.
The achievements that Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit led to build in this temple include temples, flagpoles, fences, gates, school annexes, schools, Buddhist schools, statues of patriotic heroes, as well as concrete roads and many other achievements. Others serve both the Buddhist and imperial spheres.
Currently, this pagoda is organized by the Ministry of Culture and the Ministry of Tourism as a model tourist temple. Obey traffic rules, drive home safely! 8 killed, 25 injured in road accidents nationwide on September 16
