The glory of Buddhism brings great joy and admiration for the devotees of the Dharma of the Samma Samputa and who used to send destiny to the temple for service.
But at the same time, according to my observation, when going to the pagoda during the Kan Ben festival, whether old or young, to the pagoda, some follow each other, give the monks food, count the sand mounds, and then leave the pagoda. When asked about this worship, some said that they followed the elders, but did not know the true meaning of the ceremony.
Not only the content of the Kan Ben or Pchum festival, which Khmer Buddhists are not very knowledgeable, in the content of the Dharma of many monks, Khmer Buddhists are not very knowledgeable, or it can be said that there are not many scholars.
The understanding of the Buddha Dharma of the Khmer Buddhists today is very different from the previous society. The difference is that Cambodians before the poor districts, many Cambodians go to study literature and other knowledge from the temple, so there is also an opportunity to get the knowledge of the Dharma from the teachers, most of whom are monks.
But for now, Tes district is progressing, the acquisition of knowledge does not depend entirely on the temple as before. Therefore, the distance between the Khmer people and the shadow of the Dharma is even farther.
Looking back at the temple, we see that although there is a lot of growth in the village, but it does not seem to play a role as a place to educate the Khmer people as before. The temple is only a place for worshiping the Dharma of grandparents, while young or young people go to the pagoda only when there are big traditional ceremonies such as Pchum, New Year and so on. In particular, even the Vesak festival, a major festival in Buddhism, is rarely attended by young people.
On the other hand, in the temples, we see that there is a lot of construction of temples, halls and other achievements. But there seems to be a lack of space to build or prepare a place to hold the rules of the scriptures, the decrees, the Dharma or the Trinity.
The Dharma of Preah Samma Samput has contributed significantly to the moral education in living away from the wickedness of the Cambodian people in addition to the laws of the state.
Therefore, consideration and attention should be given to strengthening the Dharma education of the Samma Samputa. Monasteries should increase the display of rules, scriptures, and Dharma orders as much as possible. There should be a program to provide knowledge of Dharma to the Khmer people in schools, temples and villages.
