Ministry of Health: The health of monks suspected of having the flu in Banteay Meanchey province has gradually improved and is waiting for the results of the test for Chik and Kovid 19
The Secretary of State confirmed that so far, the health of the 26 monks who are receiving treatment has no serious symptoms and has gradually improved. The results of the dengue test confirmed that there was no dengue fever, but were waiting for the results of the dengue test from the Institut Pasteur du Cambodge and the Kovid-19 virus from the National Institute of Public Health in Phnom Penh.
She stated that on September 14, 2020, the Ministry of Health received a report from the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Health Department about a suspected case of influenza among 26 monks living in Boeung Tros pagoda in the village. Boeung Tros, Phnom Touch commune, Mongkul Borei district, Banteay Meanchey province, which has health problems such as fever, headache and dizziness, and a total of 48 monks living in this pagoda.
According to the medical team, in addition to the monks who were admitted to the Thmar Koul Referral Hospital, there are 13 other monks who have similar health problems that the doctors suspect have a fever. Influenza and sent 13 monks with health problems to be treated at the Cambodia-Japan Friendship Hospital in Mongkol Borei at around 20:45 on September 14, 2020.
The doctors then performed the following important tasks:
* Educated and disseminated measures to prevent the spread of dengue fever, especially measures to prevent the transmission of Kovid-19 virus and influenza virus.
* Donated masks, alcohol, water, gel and soap for hand washing to the pagoda for use in the above-mentioned pagodas and recommended the implementation of prevention to prevent the spread of such contagious diseases.
* Distributed mosquito nets, sprayed with mosquito repellent, health education to help eliminate mosquito nets and put abattoirs to kill water worms to prevent the formation of tiger mosquitoes, which are agents of dengue fever and chickenpox.
* Continue to send health education messages in villages, communes and in pagodas, especially to implement measures to prevent the transmission of Kovid-19 virus, influenza virus, dengue fever and chickenpox as directed by the Ministry of Health To be consistent.
* Continue to research and monitor the health of monks living in pagodas and provide medical examination and treatment of sick monks in hospitals.
Dengue test results confirm that there is no dengue fever, but are waiting for the results of the dengue fever test from the Institut Pasteur du Cambodge and the Kovid-19 virus from the National Institute of Public Health in Phnom Penh. So far, the health of the 26 monks who are receiving treatment has no serious symptoms and is gradually improving.
