Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications successfully completes capacity building course for sub-national officials in 24 provinces
(Phnom Penh): In order to strengthen the capacity of officials at the Department of Posts and Telecommunications (MPT) in 24 provinces across the country, set up in 4 areas, a practical training workshop was organized by the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications under the initiative. Of Mr. Chea Vandeth, Minister of Posts.
Please be informed that since August 10, 2020, the capacity building workshop at the provincial level, led by a Secretary of State, representing the Minister in each area, has been held at the following locations:
3: Location: Siem Reap Province (Area 3): Preah Vihear, Stung Treng, Mondulkiri, Ratanakiri, Kratie, from August 31 to September 4, 2020, with 57 participants.
4: Location of Kampong Cham Province (Area 4): Svay Rieng, Prey Veng, Kampong Cham, Tbong Khmum, Kampong Thom, Kandal Province. From September 7 to 11, 2020, 56 participants completed the last session after the first session. Was first launched in Battambang province.
During the workshop, each area will be presented with a presentation by the trainer as a leader and experienced professional officers in the specialized unit to present the basics of knowledge related to the actual work. Daily for participants who have always faced the same problems.
Participants from the four areas raised questions to participate in discussions and shared experiences between officials and departmental officials and the Department of Exercise in front of the trainers who presented and answered the needs of the participants and under the supervision of the participants. From the leaders in charge of each area and the team.
Subjects or topics related to: Administration and Finance, Human Resources, Audit, Inspection, Postal, Legal, Regulatory, Postal, Business Administration, Telecommunications, Resource Management, Spectrum, Frequency, Radio, Communication and Information Technology, and related legal documents. Each major, including international relations or international cooperation, for a general understanding of the ministry's areas of expertise.
In the course of the workshop on the postal sector, participants were interested in issues related to local deposit business and tips on how to deal with negligence in operator management and the need for updates on relevant legal documents for implementation.
In terms of administration, finance, inspection and internal audit, the participants focused on the procedures and procedures for the use of employment permits, laws, incentives and income management.
In particular, the telecommunications sector has made the participants pay close attention to the issue, highlighting the relevant issues encountered, such as the type of telecommunication crime, the work of antenna tower management, as well as solutions to technically incorrect views on electromagnetic fields. And poor service or lack of coverage in some areas from 3G and 4G mobile services.
In the field of Information and Communication Technology (ICT), the participants are interested in the management of ICT business, which needs to be aware of the improper compliance or cooperation of ICT business operators in accordance with the legal standards.
In this regard, the participants raised opinions related to the provision of public services and response to the needs of telecommunications and ICT operators. In addition, participants enjoyed the use of simple smartphone technology that has contributed to efficient and time-saving work, especially during the Kovid-19 outbreak, such as Zoom.
Overall, after the training was completed in all 4 areas, all participants expressed their satisfaction with the organization of knowledge through this mechanism by the Minister of Posts and Telecommunications, who took care to achieve. Such workshops are designed to help immediately address the challenges that the department encounters on the job.
As a special opportunity provided to sub-national civil servants to understand the requirements of the regulations related to the duties of the Ministry that the Department must comply with, make the trainees have the opportunity to learn and understand what is still there. Lack and even give the right to the officials of the department to ask him what he wants to know and get answers that can be applied.
For the unresolved issues, the workshop highlighted the need for the leadership as well as the Ministry to have a mechanism to continue to address, such as the need to update and create additional relevant legal documents in a timely manner.
