As of today, the "Cambodian Spirit" campaign handed over 3,730 cases of Cambodian pure water to joint police officers
(Phnom Penh): The "Cambodian Spirit" campaign, initiated by Pure Water Cambodia, continues its charitable mission by donating 3,730 cases of Cambodian pure water to the joint police force across the country as of today.
There are about 17,000 cases left to be distributed to law enforcement forces.
The handing over of Cambodian pure water to the mixed police will continue until more than 20,000 cases are completed.
This mission aims to share the love and gratitude to the joint police force, especially those who strive to perform their duties on a daily basis.
In the past, the Cambodian Motivation Campaign has also supported large hospitals, joint police officers and people suffering from famine.
The Cambodian Motivation Campaign for Joint Police started in early August 2020 and will continue until the end of December 2020.
Advisor to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng and his wife holding Ben at Wat Botum Sakor
(Kandal): Mr. Oknha Pha Sok, Dr. Keang Seng, Advisor to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and his wife and family on September 14, 2020, attended the 12th Ben Ceremony at Wat Botum Sakor, a poor pagoda. In Kandal province.
On the occasion of Kan Ben, Oknha Phasok, Dr. Keang Seng, who serves as the Supreme Advisor to Samdech Preah Moha Sangkha Reach Tep Vong and CEO of Keang Seng Simon International (Teuk Nhep Tep) and his wife and family, gave alms to God. The monks have noodles, fish sauce, fish sauce, soy sauce, rice, other food items and a budget of 6 million riels.
Kan Ben and Pchum Ben ceremonies are a sublime tradition of the Khmer Buddhists. This festival not only contributes to the support of Khmer Buddhism, but also Khmer people believe that the festival in time dedicated to the merits of the ancestors who died.
Oknha Phasok Dr. Keang Seng is also a devout Buddhist. On the occasion of Kan Ben, he and his family went to Ben at 12 pagodas, bringing gifts and money to the monks to contribute to the support of Buddhism.
