(Phnom Penh): At the Office of the Council of Ministers, a meeting was held for Cambodian Muslim health professionals and IsDB Scholarship students in Cambodia. The purpose of this meeting is to help strengthen the capacity of trained doctors to be able to find jobs in line with the growing health sector and the need for truly qualified people.
The ceremony will be held on Friday afternoon, September 11, 2020 under the chairmanship of Mr. Osman Hassan, Senior Minister in charge of Special Mission and Representative of the Islamic Development Bank (ISDB) in Cambodia, with the participation of members of the National Assembly, the Royal Government, the Secretary of State, the Secretary of State, doctors and medical students. More than 250 people.
Speaking on the occasion, Senior Minister Osman Hassan reminded the establishment of the Islamic Development Bank (ISDB) that the bank was established by OIC member countries with the aim of providing scholarships to member countries only for small, less populous countries such as Cambodia. Only two or three people a year, but later he also asked for local scholarships to make Cambodia have more scholarship students.
"IsDB is appreciating the successful Cambodian scholarship students who have worked in a number of major government institutions. We are very proud of our community for benefiting from the country's peaceful development," he said. Ours is from no to less, more to more, especially during the time of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, who gave a policy of harmonization to all of us.
Finally, Senior Minister Osman Hassan reminded all doctors to unite to continue the journey towards the goal of adhering to the view that doctors do not divide, doctors must save people.
At the same time, Mr. Sos Moussin, Member of the National Assembly, said that the Islamic community is very proud to have contributed to the Royal Government since 1979, the government has rebuilt what has been lost, even with minimal human resources. Remains from slaughter in the black-shirt regime.
"So far, our doctors are close to 300, which is a small number compared to the more than 800,000 people in our community," he said. All doctors must strive to increase their capacity to respond to the job market, such as helping society, and hope that all of you will be strong successors.
On that occasion, the MP also called on doctors who graduated through the IsDB Scholarship to continue to support the bank to continue to help the next generation.
