According to a WCS press release, the images were taken by the WCS Crocodile Nest Protection Team during a patrol in collaboration with the Fisheries Administration and the Koh Kong Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
The team found 15 baby crocodiles, each about 30 centimeters long, swimming in a natural lake of wetlands, an important habitat for the species. The team noted that due to less rain this year, the lake in the wetland has become shallower, affecting its habitat over the past few years.
The crocodile is listed on the IUCN Red List as the world's most endangered species. Scientists have found its presence throughout Southeast Asia, but its presence is now almost extinct in Vietnam-Thailand and few in Laos and Indonesia. According to previous studies, there are about 5,000 crocodiles in Cambodia, which is more than any other country.
Crocodiles are facing a number of threats, including: habitat loss and illegal hunting of juvenile crocodiles, and the collection of their young and eggs for sale to crocodile farms in Cambodia, Vietnam and Thailand.
WCS has been implementing a number of conservation programs to prevent the species from becoming extinct. The project includes a group of former hunters to help find and protect nests in nature. Through law enforcement, joint patrols, including fisheries administration officials and local community members, have been collecting illegal fishing gear that could catch crocodiles and prevent the destruction of their habitat.
