When asked what the Japanese government should focus on under the new Prime Minister, 37 economists in a Sept. 2-10 survey chose to prevent and address the crisis. Twenty-eight people have chosen the digital economy direction in society and companies.
The COVID-19 crisis reflects technological shortcomings in governments and companies, many of whom still adhere to a paper-based work culture, which experts say is seriously undermining productivity. Twenty-two economists chose to create jobs and boost domestic demand, while 13 others chose foreign policy and security.
Of all the economists surveyed, 30 predicted that Japan's economy would shrink by 6% by 2020, the largest slowdown since 1994. The worst-case scenario analysis showed that the Japanese economy could shrink by 8.1%.
