(Phnom Penh): General Him Yan, Deputy General Commissioner of the National Police in charge of traffic work, this morning, September 08, 2020, claimed that during each festival, the traffic situation was very busy and at the same time there were traffic accidents. Increase as well. Previous figures show that at least seven people were killed in road accidents during festivals.
Speaking to reporters after the meeting between the leaders of the Ministry of Public Works and the leadership of the National Police, General Him Yan pointed out that during the recent flood, 10 people were killed and more than 40 injured due to accidents. Traffic.
General Him Yan said, "Please note that every day there is an increase in traffic, there is an increase in accidents, such as the day of the flood, a few days ago, 10 people were killed and more than 40 injured a day." In addition, during the New Year (2019), as well as Pchum Ben last year, at least an average of seven people died a day and injured at least 20 people due to traffic accidents.
In order to reduce traffic accidents on the occasion of the upcoming Pchum Ben festival, General Him Yan said that the National Police General Commission will set out measures to guide and implement the police force across the country to protect security and traffic safety. The general added that the National Committee for Road Safety will issue a call to the people on the use of the road.
At the same time, General Him Yan also presented the results of the enforcement of road traffic law, since the implementation date of May 1, 2020 until now, reduced traffic accidents more than 600 times, reduced the number of fatalities. More than 200 people were injured and more than 1,000 were injured. This is a great result that Cambodia can reduce the loss of life and damage to people's property.
Taking this opportunity, the Deputy General Commissioner also commended the many consumers who have participated in obeying the traffic law better, which makes the driving environment more orderly and safer.
The General Commission of the National Police also aims to make more efforts to improve both traffic law enforcement and law education to the people. This is what General Him Yan, Deputy General Commissioner of the National Police, emphasized.
