Earlier on Tuesday, China accused Indian troops of violating bilateral agreements and fired warning shots into the air during a confrontation with Chinese troops along the disputed border on Monday amid tensions between the two countries. Both countries have risen again.
The Chinese military spokesman for the Western Region, Zhang Shuili, said in a statement posted on the Chinese military's official website on Tuesday morning that the Chinese border guards Immediate retaliation was taken to keep the situation stable. However, China did not specify what exactly measures would be taken.
However, in response, the Indian military issued a statement dismissing the allegations from Beijing, stating that "the People's Liberation Army (PLA) has violated the bilateral agreement and continues to act aggressively while "Military, diplomatic and political talks at all levels are moving forward."
The statement added that the Chinese army tried to approach the Indian army base in the conflict zone Ladakh, and when confronted with the Indian army, the Chinese army fired warning shots into the air, which was an act of aggression. The agreement bans armed clashes in the border area.
In June, Chinese and Indian troops clashed once, killing 20 Indian soldiers in a series of shootings. After that, China and India decided to deploy more troops along the border. The Chinese side also acknowledged that its troops were killed and wounded, but did not release figures.
