(Indochina): After the Lanchang Empire split into three kingdoms, the whole of Laos fell under Siamese rule for 115 years, from 1778-1893 AD, but from the end of the 19th century until the middle of the 19th century. In the 20th century, Laos also became a French colony.
While the Siamese were still controlling Laos, Vientiane was suddenly invaded by the Great Chinese army until it conquered and ruled Laos for a while.
In 1858, French colonists became interested and wanted to expand their power into the Mekong, sending Henri Mouhut (rediscoverer of Angkor temples) to travel along the Mekong to find their way. Doing business with South China in order to compete with the British colonies that took control of Burma at the same time.
Before occupying Laos, the French Empire controlled the territory of Kampuchea Krom and the whole of Cambodia in 1863 and controlled the whole of Vietnam in 1883.
As for Laos, it could not escape from these large colonies, because if it fled to the west it would meet Siam, if it fled to the east or south it would meet France, which controlled Cambodia and Vietnam, and if it fled to the north it would. Meet the British who controlled Burma and parts of the Great Wall. But in the end, Laos also fell under French rule.
Pavi ordered the French navy to close all shipping lanes in the Gulf of Thailand to prevent commercial and naval activities. France did so in order to force the Bangkok palace to return Laos to its colony.
