Stakeholders known, including village chiefs, commune chiefs and a small number of officials in Samlot district, Battambang province, who are known to be behind the preparation of people to encroach on forest land must stop immediately because this is an act. Illegal, while local authorities have a role to play in protecting and conserving their local natural resources.
On September 5, 2020, professional officers of Battambang Provincial Authority cracked down on and confiscated two tractors that cleared the forest land of Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary in Kampong Lpou Commune, Samlot District, Battambang Province on an area of nearly 200 hectares.
After the crackdown on the encroachment on the forest land and the arrest of the driver and two tractors, the people were organized to protest to release the perpetrators and take the tractor back, and even demanded that the forest land be cleared and occupied.
This shows that despite the efforts of the provincial administration and specialized departments to crack down on and prevent natural resource crimes on a regular basis, there are still criminals who conspire and commit crimes to encroach on land. According to the law, no one has the right to buy or sell land in a protected area, and it is illegal for village and commune authorities to sign and buy land in a protected area.
Corrupt people must stop the culture of using people to clear, encroach on, illegally occupy state forest land and ask for land clearing. This is an illegal act and contrary to the principles set by Samdech Techo Hun Sen on July 3 and 5, 2020. On July 5, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen posted on his Facebook page that "those who take advantage of the division and receive land will be punished."
