This statement was made while she, along with Duke Ing Sisrun and government officials and Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda, attended the third Kan Ben shift at Vihear Samnor pagoda in Vihear Luong village, Vihear Luong commune, Ponhea Leu district, Kandal province on Saturday morning. Gender Chhnang Chuot Tosak P.S. 2564 is September 5, 2020.
The Kan Ben Pchum Ben festival is celebrated by Cambodian Buddhists in order to prepare monks for the rainy season because it rains during the rainy season, making it difficult for monks to perform Ben Bath. On the other hand, to dedicate merit to the souls of their ancestors to go to death.
In the third Kan Ben ceremony at Wat Vihear Samnor and all the Buddhists brought various food offerings to the monks to dedicate to the ancestors who passed away to the next world, as well as to the merit to All patriotic ancestors came to accept the merits of Buddhism according to the state religion.
It should be noted that Kan Ben Pchum Ben is a major national Buddhist festival in the Kingdom of Cambodia. This year, the Kan Ben Pchum Ben Festival is celebrated from the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach, the year of Chuot Tosak BE 2564, which is from 3rd to 17th September 2020.
Ms. Kittisongkhab Dr. Men Sam An Participates in the Third Ben Ceremony at Wat Vihear Samnor in Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province
(Kandal): Ms. Kittisangkhab Dr. Men Sam An, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Assembly-Senate Relations and Inspection, together with Duke Ing Sisrun and other government officials and Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda attended the third Kan Ben ceremony at Wat Vihear Samnor in the village. Vihear Luong, Vihear Luong commune, Ponhea Leu district, Kandal province, on Saturday morning, 2564 is September 5, 2020.
The Kan Ben Pchum Ben festival is celebrated by Cambodian Buddhists in order to prepare monks for the rainy season because it rains during the rainy season, making it difficult for monks to perform Ben Bath. On the other hand, to dedicate merit to the souls of their ancestors to go to death.
Finally, Ms. Kittisanghabandit, along with the Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda, also invited the monks to count the bowls and offer prayers to the ceremony.
