(Battambang): After the Battambang Cloth Market reopened, on September 3, 2020, the Battambang Provincial Administration led by Mr. Nop Dara and Mr. Soeum Bunrith, Deputy Provincial Governor, represented by Mr. Nguon Rattanak, Provincial Governor and his colleagues inspected See the work of repairing and cutting stalls at Battambang Cloth Market, which was burnt down on August 30, 2020.
After the fire caused 33 out of 335 stalls to be completely damaged, all the leaders and relevant officials of Battambang province tried to organize the stalls as well as the order to clean and clean and repair as a solution. The uncles and aunts, who were the affected traders, soon resumed their business.
On the occasion of inspecting the work of cutting stalls at Battambang Cloth Market after reopening, Mr. Nop Dara and Mr. Soeum Bunrith met with traders. Taking this opportunity, Mr. Nop Dara also donated oranges with his own money to distribute to traders and motodup people to eat.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts on September 4, 2020, issued a new statement stating that the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum has received the JIKJI Award of UNESCO World Memory 2020.
On August 17, 2020, the Paris-based UNESCO World Heritage Committee decided to award the JIKJI of UNESCO World Memory to the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum for its achievements in "Preservation of documents and archives of photographs is an important historical document for humanity."
The ministry said the decision to award the JIKJI was made by a panel of judges selected by the International Advisory Committee of the World Memory of UNESCO.
The purpose of this program is to promote and encourage individuals or institutions that have worked hard to protect and preserve the heritage, focusing on education, training, dissemination and knowledge sharing to people of all races and ages. The UNESCO JIKJI World Memory Award is given to individuals and public institutions every two years.
The Tuol Sleng Museum of Genocide of the Kingdom of Cambodia is the eighth institution to receive the 2020 Prize. The main basis for the award is based on the preservation of documents, archives, photos and the organization of a common database that makes it easy for the public to find documents.
The Ministry of Culture added that the award is a testament to the spirit of the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, working with international partners to preserve documents as evidence for the whole of humanity, study and learn to prevent genocide. Race is re-emerging in Cambodia and the world.
