On the occasion of the first day of Kanben, Mr. Chea Chanto and Mrs. Banve donated 9 million and 2 hundred thousand Riels, which came from pure heart. Monks also live in these three monasteries. Ministry of Tourism: Tourism business reopens 30% nationwide
(Phnom Penh): The Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Tourism, Mr. Tith Chantha, said that during the Kovid-19 disease, almost 90% of tourism service businesses nationwide were shut down. However, due to the recent improvement in domestic tourists, about 30% of tourism businesses have reopened.
The statement was made by senior officials of the Ministry of Tourism at a press conference on the state of tourism during the Kovid 19 crisis, organized by the Government Spokesperson's Office on the morning of September 3, 2020 at the Commission's office. Minister.
Throughout Cambodia, there are a total of 9,689 tourism business bases, including hotels, guesthouses, restaurants, massage spas, travel agencies, karaoke, bars, discos, teak, beer gardens, sports tourism and more.
On that occasion, Mr. Tith Chantha said that during the crisis in Cambodia, 19 hotels, guesthouses, restaurants, restaurants, as well as other tourism business establishments, almost 90% were suspended due to prevention. Kovid spread and due to the lack of tourists.
He said that at present, although international tourism activities have been suspended almost completely, the tourism movement in Cambodia has improved, with about 30% of the total tourism service business bases across the country reopened.
Other tourism business bases, which have been suspended in the past and have not yet opened, are also set to reopen in the near future.
According to the Ministry of Tourism, the reopening of the tourism service business base has alleviated the difficulties of workers and staff in the tourism sector to return to a large number, even if not. Full nationwide.
As Tith Chantha expects, in the future, if the Kovid-19 disease problem is reduced or better managed, international tourists will gradually return to visit Cambodia and the tourism business base. Will fully function across the country again or may have further progress.
Mr. Kun Kim: Disaster management and response work is a joint work that requires the participation of all stakeholders
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, said that disaster management and response work is not a separate task of the National Committee for Disaster Management, but a joint venture. Of the nation, which requires the participation of all ministries, institutions and all stakeholders to respond to this disaster work effectively.
He said this in the inter-ministerial meeting to approve the National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023 on September 3, 2020.
The meeting was chaired by Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management, High Representative of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Mr. Chrea Sochenda, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Secretariat for Disaster Management, Office of the Council of Ministers. His Excellency Deputy Prime Minister Ben Chin, Minister of the Office of the Council of Ministers.
The meeting was also attended by representatives of senior officials in the Secretariat for Disaster Management in the ministries-institutions that are members of the National Committee for Disaster Management.
Based on the national plan and the high sense of responsibility for solving people's problems in the event of a disaster, Mr. Kun Kim requested all stakeholders to be prepared to allocate budget and other resources in the action plan and plan. Annual budget for work, risk reduction, response and rehabilitation on a regular basis in their respective areas of expertise.
In today's meeting, the stakeholders agreed to approve the overall content of the National Action Plan for Disaster Risk Reduction 2019-2023.
In this planning process, the most important factor is the participation of all relevant ministries and institutions in stages. The plan has been delayed until now due to the Kovit-19 issue, and another important factor is the collection of input from relevant ministries and institutions to ensure the completeness and ownership of the national plan. .
