Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch Although not a senior leader in Democratic Kampuchea, Duch was the head of Tuol Sleng prison, which involved the torture and killing of more than 12,000 people.
Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch was born on November 17, 1942 in Pov Veay village, Peam Bang commune, Stong district, Kampong Thom province. Duch was arrested and detained on 10 May 1999 and transferred to the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) on 30 July 2007. Crimes against humanity carry a life sentence.
On February 3, 2012, the Supreme Court Chamber sentenced Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch to life in prison. He was sent to Kandal Provincial Prison on June 6, 2013 to serve his sentence.
As of September 2, 2020, Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch was detained at Kandal Provincial Prison for 7 years, 2 months and 25 days.
Duch's death led the victims and their families during the Khmer Rouge regime, as well as the inmates at Tuol Sleng Prison, to express different views and interests.
Some agreed to vote for Duch. But for others, the scale of the torture left behind by the Khmer regime and the torture they received under Duch's direct command in Tuol Sleng was unforgettable, and they still suffer from Duch's atrocities. The victims, who could not forget these pains, still could not forgive Duch even after his death.
Duch's death left a message that leaders who committed atrocities like the Khmer Rouge, not to mention hated while still alive, did not remain hated and intolerant. General Mak Chito Meets Australian Police in Cambodia to Strengthen Cooperation
