(Phnom Penh): Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch, although not a senior leader in the Democratic Kampuchea regime, Duch was the head of Tuol Sleng prison, which was involved in the torture and killing of more than 12,000 people.
Duch, originally named Kaing Guek Eav, was born on November 17, 1942 in Pov Veay Village, Peam Bang Commune, Stong District, Kampong Thom Province. Duch, a Chinese hybrid, has five siblings in a poor peasant family.
After failing the exam in Kampong Thom, Duch came to study at a famous high school in Phnom Penh, Preah Sisowath High School and stayed at Wat Ounalom.
In 1964, Kaing Guek Eav passed the exam and became one of the best students in the country in mathematics. Kaing Guek Eav then went to study at the Pedagogical Institute near the Independence Monument.
In 1965, Kaing Guek Eav went to work as a teacher of mathematics, physics and chemistry at Skun High School in Kampong Thom province. People who used to study with Kaing Guek Eav said that Kaing Guek Eav is a teacher who is kind, friendly, not arrogant and close to students, which makes students love everyone.
While studying in Phnom Penh, Kaing Guek Eav, a descendant of a poor farmer, saw a wide gap between rich and poor. It was then that Kaing Guek Eav began to hate the feudal class and fall in love with the communist ideology.
On the other hand, while studying at the Kaing Guek Eav Pedagogical Institute, he met one of the most important communists, Son Sen, who was then the director of the institute and later became the Minister of Defense of the Khmer Rouge regime.
In 1967, while working as a teacher in Kampong Thom, Kaing Guek Eav became a member of the Communist Party of Kampuchea and got to know Seng Hoeun, who later became Duch's assistant at Tuol Sleng Prison.
In the same year, Kaing Guek Eav was arrested by the Sangkum Reastr Niyum government police and taken to Prey Sar prison in Phnom Penh. In Kaing Guek Eav, he met Mam Nay, also known as Chan, who later became Duch's deputy director at Tuol Sleng.
Although the Khmer Rouge did not come to power at the time, the prisons occupied by Duch were located in liberated areas, so they were under the full control of the Khmer Rouge. Tens of thousands of prisoners are being held in both prisons. It was then that Duch began experimenting with how to torture and interrogate prisoners.
While fighting in the forest, Duch married Chhim Sophal, also known as Rom, who lived in a village near Duch's place of work.
After the Khmer Rouge came to power on April 17, 1975, Duch was appointed director of the S-21 Security Department, known as Tuol Sleng Prison in Phnom Penh.
According to documents at Tuol Sleng prison, which are currently kept by the Duchess of the Cambodian Documentation Center, Son Sen alias Khieu, a former Duch teacher at the Pedagogical Institute, and Nuon Chea alias Bong Nuon, deputy secretary of the Communist Party of Kampuchea. These documents include Duch's signature on the transcript and Duch's note ordering his subordinates to torture and kill prisoners.
Between 1978 and early 1979, the Khmer Rouge intensified its internal purge operations. During that time, a large number of Khmer Rouge cadres were arrested and sent to Duch for torture and interrogation at Tuol Sleng Prison. Duch has compiled a number of documents on the biographies and confessions of prisoners. These documents became the main evidence in the Khmer Rouge tribunal's investigation.
After the Khmer Rouge was overthrown on January 7, 1979, Duch was one of the last Khmer Rouge cadres to flee Phnom Penh. Duch arrived in Thailand in May 1979.
Duch went to reunite with his family in the forest in Samlot district. Duch was removed by his second brother, Nuon Chea, on the grounds that he had not destroyed all the documents at Tuol Sleng Prison before fleeing Phnom Penh. Duch later became an English and math teacher at a school in a refugee camp in Thailand.
In 1986, Duch was sent by the Khmer Rouge leaders to work as a Khmer language teacher at the Institute of Foreign Languages in Beijing, China. A year later, Duch returned to Thailand and changed his name to Hang Pin. Duch went to work at Pol Pot's office in Camp 505.
This decision led to a strong reaction from the victims of Tuol Sleng as well as the victims of the Khmer Rouge regime. This dissatisfaction only led the prosecutor to appeal. The Supreme Court Chamber ruled on 3 February 2012 to sentence Duch to life in prison.
On June 6, 2013, Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch was sent to Kandal Provincial Prison to serve his remaining prison sentence.
Announcing the position of Director of Svay Rieng Provincial Department of Agriculture, Mr. Sok Sotheavuth to replace Mr. Thach Ratana who has retired
(Svay Rieng): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, this morning, September 2, 2020, presided over the inauguration of the new Director of the Svay Rieng Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries by Mr. Sok Sotheavuth. Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Svay Rieng Province, replacing Mr. Thach Ratana, who has retired.
The ceremony was also attended by Mr. Men Vibol, Governor of Svay Rieng Province and the accompanying delegation, Mr. and Mrs. Deputy Provincial Governor, Mr. President of the Provincial Court, Mr. Preah Reacha of the Provincial Prosecutor's Office, Mr. Director of Administration of the Provincial Hall, representatives of the three Armed Forces. And other relevant officials, a total of 96 people were present.
Mr. Men Vibol stated that due to the factors of peace and political stability in the country, which are the fruits of the win-win policy and the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia provides a good opportunity. Good for the development of the country as well as Svay Rieng to grow in all areas.
He said that after the fall of the Pol Pot regime, Svay Rieng quickly became an important industrial province after the Royal Government decided to establish a special economic zone in the territory of Svay Rieng in 2005, which provided significant benefits. Much guarantees to improve the living standards of the people from year to year.
At the same time, he reminded the department leaders at all levels and professional officers to pay attention to increase the management capacity, knowledge, skills and experience, and in the context of the situation of Kovid 19 disease, the Minister also recommended the key points. Some such as:
* First, to continue the tradition of good cooperation with local authorities, relevant professional institutions at all levels and agricultural development partners to improve.
* Second: To strengthen and improve the management and development of human resources to be more professional and efficient in work.
* Third: Transfer modern technology to farmers, encourage the implementation of agricultural production under the contract, improve the quality of agricultural products to compete and pay attention to good agricultural practices with intelligence.
* Fourth: Take measures to prevent all kinds of crimes in all sub-sectors in a timely manner. Raise the hygiene standards of slaughterhouses as well as the sanitary phytosanity of other agricultural products, both domestic and imported, to ensure public welfare.
* Fifth: Continue to encourage people to grow crops that are useful to supply to the market to survive during the impact of Kovid 19.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, on September 2, 2020, stated that among the countries in the ASEAN region, Cambodia is the last country to be approved to use the law on consumer protection.
This statement was made on the occasion of Minister Pan Sosak's inauguration of the National Report on "Consumer Awareness Level in Cambodia" and "ASEAN Consumer Awareness Project" on the morning of September 2. This year 2020.
Mr. Pan Sosak said that this law aims to ensure the protection of consumers and participate in promoting fair competition, aims to set rules and mechanisms to protect the rights and interests of consumers, build trust between consumers and traders to Contribute to creating an environment of fair business and especially raising the standard of living of the general population towards poverty reduction in Cambodia.
In addition, Minister Pan Sosak emphasized that consumer protection is a multi-sectoral task, requiring the participation of relevant ministries and institutions in areas such as health, agriculture, finance, industry, banking, technology and more. Telecommunications, etc.
He said that for this reason, the Ministry of Commerce, as the coordinator of consumer protection, has been preparing a draft sub-decree on the establishment of the National Committee for Consumer Protection, which is represented by relevant ministries and institutions responsible for Consumer protection in each sector works together in order to protect the legitimate interests of consumers.
In addition, the Ministry of Commerce, which has the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Anti-Fraud as its staff, has been discussing the preparation of relevant proclamations and legal documents under the Consumer Protection Law to support law enforcement activities. Strong and highly efficient.
The Minister further stated that with the excellent cooperation under the project "Consumer Protection in ASEAN", the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Fraud Suppression of the Ministry of Commerce has received technical support and Finance to conduct a national survey on "Consumer Awareness Level in Cambodia".
According to the preliminary report of the survey, although a total of 200 samples were small to represent consumers across Cambodia, but were selected from a mix of women and men, urban and rural users in the four provinces. Phnom Penh, Siem Reap, Battambang and Kampong Cham provinces cover the main geographical regions of Cambodia.
The final product of this survey is the production of a national report on "Consumer Awareness in Cambodia" and he encourages and encourages the entire workshop to review, discuss, provide additional input and use the report. This:
* A basic document in consumer protection * A document in support of the reflection and promotion of public knowledge * A basic document in the development of consumer protection response plans in Cambodia.
