General Neth Savoeun Introduces 8 Measures to Maintain Security and Order on the Occasion of Kan Ben and Pchum Ben
(Phnom Penh): General Dr. Neth Savoeun, General Commissioner of the National Police, on September 1, 2020, issued instructions on measures to protect security, order and social security during the upcoming Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals. Coming soon.
In the instructions sent to the Central Department, the Department of Specialized Units and the Capital-Provincial Police Commission, General Dr. Neth Savoeun introduced the following 8 main points:
* First, pay attention to increase the grasp and analysis of the situation and information related to security and social order, especially acts of terrorism, crime and other illegal activities to take measures to prevent and suppress effectively in a timely manner.
* Second, to promote the implementation of the policy of "safe villages and communes" and to carefully manage all kinds of subjects in the village-commune community, using all measures to eliminate the effectiveness first. Do not allow the subject to have the opportunity to act in a way that causes insecurity or endangers life and endangers the property of the people.
* Third: Take strict measures to protect all goals of celebrating festivals and entertainment purposes such as pagodas, temples, temples, resorts, as well as increase security and ensure the safety and security of the King and the top leaders of the nation. Come to the festival.
At the same time, care must be taken to patrol and protect the houses and property of the people who travel to their hometowns or to relax in various areas with their families.
* Fourth: We must continue to pay attention to the implementation of the road traffic law in the spirit of Sub-Decree No. 39, dated March 17, 2020, especially to thoroughly inspect all the original targets.
At the same time, it is necessary to maintain order on national roads, towns, and entrances to the capital-province, to reduce congestion or accidents that may occur eventually, and to cooperate with major media outlets to disseminate up-to-date information. About traffic jams so that people know and turn around.
* Sixth: We must pay attention to educating the people to be aware of fires and disasters and to strengthen the command work at all levels to prepare for timely intervention. At the same time, there must be a change of shifts to ensure that all police forces take a break to participate in the ceremony.
* Seventh: Prepare delegates and gifts to visit the police force on the battlefield, the force is being treated at the hospital, home and family of police officers who were sacrificed or injured in the cause of national defense.
* Eighth: Add the situation and results of operations during Kanben and Pchum Ben into two different, from Ben 1 to Pchum Ben and for 3 days, Pchum Ben report to the National Police. September 18, 2020 is mandatory.
In 1963, when Xi was 10 years old, his father was expelled from the party and sent to work in a factory in Luoyang, Henan Province. In May 1966, his secondary education was cut short by the Cultural Revolution, when all high school classes were suspended to allow students to criticize and evaluate their teachers.
The Xi Jinping family home was destroyed by student fighters, and his sister Xi Heping was killed. His mother was later forced into public trial as he was escorted in front of a crowd considered an enemy of the revolution.
A few months later, unable to cope with rural life, Bansi fled to Beijing. He was captured during the battle to get rid of the refugees from the countryside and sent to a labor camp to dig a pit. He later became the party secretary of a farming group and resigned in 1975.
From 1975 to 1979, Xi Jinping studied chemical engineering at the prestigious Tsinghua University in Beijing as a student "worker, farmer and soldier." Those who study engineering spend one-fifth of their time studying Marxist-Leninist theory, Mao Zedong theory, agricultural work, and the People's Liberation Army.
In 1985, Xi Jinping enrolled in the United States. Among the Chinese delegation to study agriculture in the United States, he was staying at the home of an American family in Massachusetts, Iowa.
From 1998 to 2002, he studied Marxist philosophy and ideological education again in the postgraduate program "By Practice" at the Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences at Xinhua University and received a PhD in Law. Degree covers the fields of law, politics, administration and revolutionary history.
On his way to senior positions, Xi Jinping joined the Chinese Communist Youth League in 1971 and applied to the Chinese Communist Party 10 times before being accepted in 1974. In 1982, he was sent to Chong Ting County in Hebei to work as the party's deputy secretary. A year later, he was promoted to party secretary to become the district's top official.
In 1997, Xi Jinping was included as a reserve member of the 15th CPC Central Committee. However, of the 151 reserve members elected at the 15th party congress, Xi received the least number of votes. He was last in the ranks of all the members, most likely because of his insignificant leadership status.
In 2002, Xi Jinping left Fujian and held several leading political positions in neighboring Xinjiang. He eventually took over as provincial party president after months of acting governorship, taking over one of the top provincial posts for the first time in his career.
Xi Jinping was sworn in as party chairman in Shanghai in March 2007 after the ouster of former chairman Chen Liangyu in September 2006 over a corruption scandal. In just seven months, he has been named chairman of one of China's most important regional parties, and this is a clear sign that he is being taken seriously by China's top leaders.
In Shanghai, Xi Jinping tried to avoid controversy and was known as a staunch supporter of party discipline. In fact, the mayor of Shanghai tried to take Xi's liver by arranging a special train to transport him from Shanghai to Hershu, but he immediately refused to take the train, citing party rules stating that the train In particular, can only be used for "national leaders".
In addition, Xi also served as a senior member of the Central Secretariat of the Communist Party. This assumption was made clear at the 11th National People's Congress in March 2008 when Xi was elected Vice President of the People's Republic of China under Ho Chi Minh's administration.
After receiving this position, Xi Jinping held many positions. He was assigned to a major organization at the 2008 Summer Olympics in Beijing and to a central government role in Hong Kong and Macau.
He also became the director of the Central Party University, the top institution for ideological education and training of Communist Party cadres. Xi Jinping is considered the "son of the party", referring to politicians who were the descendants of the first Chinese revolutionaries.
"I would put him in the Nelson Mandela class," he said. A person with great emotional stability who does not allow personal misfortunes or misery to affect his judgment. "On the other hand, he is very interesting."
* About becoming China's top leader
On November 15, 2012, Xi Jinping was elected General Secretary of the Communist Party of China and Chairman of the Central Military Commission by the 18th Party Congress. Xi became the first unofficial leader and the first leader in Chinese history to be born in the People's Republic of China.
"Our people ... expect better education, stable jobs, better incomes, reliable social security, high standards of medical care," Xi said. "Life is more comfortable and the environment is more beautiful."
Xi Jinping has also vowed to tackle corruption in the best possible way, arguing that it could be a threat to the party's survival. Xi has no intention of talking about comprehensive economic reform.
In December 2012, Xi Jinping visited Guangdong Province, his first visit outside Beijing since becoming the party's top leader. The main theme of the visit is to call for stronger and stronger economic and military reforms.
Xi visited Teng Xiaoping's statue, which is described as following in the footsteps of Teng's 1992 visit to the south. During the visit, Xi recalled his favorite slogan, "Dream of China".
"This dream can be said to be a dream of a strong nation, and when it comes to the army, it is a dream of a strong army," Xi Jinping told the people. The visit is significant in that he has left the Chinese leader's travel habits in a variety of ways.
Former Chinese leaders usually have dinner outside, but Xi and his team enjoy a buffet at the hotel. He travels in a regular car with his colleagues, rather than driving in large numbers of luxury cars, making it difficult to restrict traffic on major roads.
