Senior Minister Ly Thuch: Everything can change in less than a second for deminers who could lose their lives or become disabled
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Deputy Chief of the Mine Authority, spoke about the heroism of the demining team, who always risked their lives while on duty. He claims that for deminers, everything can change in less than a second, which can lead to loss of life or even disability.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch confirmed this at a ceremony to commemorate the victims of landmines and unexploded ordnance, especially deminers at the US Embassy in Phnom Penh on August 31, 2020. US Embassy compound.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch spoke about the Prime Minister Samdech Techo's high regard for the loss of life, disability and injuries of the people as well as deminers: "Because mines and explosive remnants of war are the saddest and saddest thing for the whole nation."
The senior minister added that Cambodia is small and poor, but Cambodia has a wide heart to participate in humanitarian and peacekeeping work in the world.
The Senior Minister expressed his gratitude and mentioned the win-win policy at the end of 1998 under the leadership of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, which brought complete peace and stability throughout Cambodia and allowed all deminers Able to carry out its humanitarian demining work everywhere without fear of participating in liberating the land and saving the lives of the people from the threat of landmines.
On behalf of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Chairman of the Mine Action Authority, the Senior Minister expressed his deep gratitude for the generous support of humanitarian mine action in Cambodia by the Government and people of the United States in contributing to the reduction of threats. Landmines and explosive remnants of war and saving lives in Cambodia since 1993 at a cost of more than $ 160 million.
On this special occasion, the Senior Minister also thanked the donors and other outstanding friends of Cambodia, including: Australia, England, Japan, Germany, Switzerland, China, Norway, South Korea, Canada, New Zealand, Ireland, UNDP and UNICEF, as well as the NGO Foundation. Civilians and philanthropists as well.
He added that we must continue to nurture and enhance the partnership with high commitment between the Royal Government, the community, donors, operators and all stakeholders to make Cambodia a mine-free vision by 2025.
From 1992 to 2020, 151 deminers from all operators were killed and injured due to the liberation of their land.
Since 1992, deminers have cleared 2,043 square kilometers of land for housing, agriculture, disaster reduction and other development work, and found and destroyed anti-personnel mines, anti-tank mines and national remnants. Millions of wars erupted, with more than 5.7 million direct and indirect beneficiaries.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Microfinance Association, emphasized that the banking and microfinance sector in Cambodia is strong despite the Kovid-19 crisis because the Cambodian people have high trust. On this field.
Kea Boran said that during Kovid-19, cash in the banking sector increased, although credit declined, but the amount of money people put into the banking and microfinance systems continued to increase. .
This statement was made by the Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association on the occasion of participating in the weekly special guest program of Fresh News TV under the theme "The situation of Cambodia's microfinance sector in the Kovid-19 crisis".
Kea Boran stated, "The public's trust in the banking and microfinance sector in Cambodia is strong, despite the Kovid-19 crisis."
"Cash in the banking sector increased despite the decline in credit, but the cash flow of people into the banking and microfinance system continued to increase in the second quarter despite the decline in credit," he said. However, such savings increase.
Mr. Kea Boran emphasized that this proves that the Cambodian people have a high level of trust and confidence in the banking and microfinance sector in Cambodia.
According to the Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association, in the first quarter of 2020, economic growth was normal, although Kovid-19 began to spread in some countries, but Cambodia will continue to normal activities until the end of March. 2020.
"We see the impact of the Kovid-19 onset in April, when the general economic situation deteriorated as cross-country travel was generally suspended and the Kovid-19 case was generally suspended," he said. There is also an increase.
Kea Boran stated that in the microfinance sector, we see the same impact as the general economy in April, May, June, which is the most affected month, especially in April, which we see credit growth. Is falling.
In addition, the chairman of the board of the MFI confirmed that growth continued to slow slightly in May, but recovered during June.
Meanwhile, Mr. Kea Boran said that Cambodia was affected by only two months, which was severe during April and May, and from June to July improved.
He added that this improvement was due to the fact that Cambodia was able to control the situation related to the Kovid-19 outbreak better, with no outbreaks in the community, and past cases of infection were all cases of imports.
Kea Boran added that Cambodia was not shut down, which made the economy difficult, and because the number of Kovid-19 was not increasing or spreading in the community.
In addition, the Royal Government has reopened a number of sectors, such as hotels, restaurants and some entertainment venues have opened more, although better capacity requirements are required.
Mr. Kea Boran stated that all these cases have made Cambodia resume activities, which has increased the microfinance sector as a whole to encourage the economy to move forward.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Microfinance Association, confirmed that as of the end of July 2020, 250,000 customers have been reorganized by the microfinance institutions that are members of the Association. About $ 1,200 million.
He said that the loan restructuring is only for customers affected by 19 Kovid, not all customers.
Mr. Boran said that the reorganization of credit has helped a lot of customers, especially those affected by Kovid 19 because business and employees in some sectors have affected their income, so if not No reorganization of the loan, both customers will face the consequences of repayment under the first contract.
He said that the reorganization of credit also helps people to have some cash to expand their business and business.
According to Kea Boran, Cambodians are educated and do not believe the call of any person or group not to return the money to the microfinance institution.
Mr. Boran said that the bad credit so far has increased slightly from about 1 percent to 2.5 percent during the Kovid 19 crisis, but a figure that is still low compared to other countries in the region. This figure does not make the banking and financial sector in Cambodia difficult.
