According to the United Nations report on economic losses, poverty and disasters in the last two decades, direct economic losses from climate-related disasters have increased to 250%. In the Lower Mekong region, between 1998 and 2017, Thailand suffered the worst damage, with an estimated $ 52.4 billion in damage.
According to the MRC, while seasonal floods bring different benefits to the region, the average annual loss from floods in the Lower Mekong Basin is estimated at $ 60 million to $ 70 million. Overall. Cambodia and Vietnam account for about two-thirds of the total annual flood damage in the Mekong region, respectively.
Recent MRC studies have also shown that flood events in the Lower Mekong Basin have increased more frequently and worsened in recent decades, and this trend will continue. The basin is also vulnerable to further climate change, including a median temperature increase of 0.8 degrees Celsius, projected by 2030.
