Mr. Pan Sosak led the delegation to attend the ASEAN Economic Ministers' Consultation with 11 stakeholders via video
The meeting was held from 27-29 August 2020. The meeting achieved the following results:
19th: The 19th ASEAN-China Economic Ministers' Consultation:
ASEAN-US Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultation examined the progress of the implementation of the ASEAN-US Trade and Investment Framework (TIFA) and the E3 Work Plan and promoted the implementation of the action plan under the TIFA 2020-2021 framework focusing on digital, transparent and transparent trade. Implementation of ASEAN Trade Dialogue Regulations and Environment, Trade Facilitation, MSME Development and so on.
During the consultation, the Minister praised the US-ASEAN Business Council's recommendations for post-Kovid-19 economic recovery through digital trade and strengthening the competitiveness of small and medium enterprises.
The Minister also welcomed US investment in ASEAN, with Cambodia ready to work with stakeholders to boost trade and interests between the two sides.
3rd: The 4th ASEAN-Hong Kong (China) Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultations agreed to enhance ASEAN-Hong Kong economic relations through the implementation of the ASEAN-Hokong Free Trade Agreement (AHKFTA) and the ASEAN-Hokong Investment Agreement (AHKIA). The ministers hope that the full implementation of the two agreements by the end of 2020 will strengthen trade and investment liberalization between the two sides.
4: The 26th ASEAN-Japan Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultations examined the progress of the implementation of the ASEAN-Japan Economic Strengthening Action Plan in response to Kovid-19, which focused on three initiatives, including: (1) maintaining close economic ties between ASEAN and Japan, (2) cutting Reduce the impact of Kovid-19 on the economy and (3) strengthen the economy.
The consultations also hoped that the full implementation of the first protocol, which amended the integration of trade, services and investment into the ASEAN-Japan Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement, would broaden ASEAN-Japan economic relations. Especially for service providers and investors to expand their business in the region.
9th: The 9th ASEAN-Russia Economic Ministers' Consultation:
6: The 23rd ASEAN Plus Three Ministerial Consultations:
The consultations also pushed for the signing of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) to be completed by 2020 to restore regional business and economic confidence.
The meeting welcomed regional initiatives to reduce the impact of Kovid disease through the establishment of the ASEAN Kovid-19 Response Fund, as well as work towards the establishment of ASEAN Regional Medical Reserves and Standard Operating Procedures for Response to public health emergencies. The consultations also expressed support for WTO reform.
9th 9th ASEAN-Canada Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultations approved the Joint Action Plan on Trade and Investment (JDTI) 2021-2025, which shows the two sides' commitment to further deepen and broaden economic ties.
The consultations also welcomed the outcome of the 4th ASEAN-Canada Trade Policy Dialogue held on 8-12 June 2020, focusing on four areas: government procurement, the environment, employment and state-owned enterprises. (SOEs). The consultations urged the two sides to continue discussing the next steps to explore the feasibility of the ASEAN-Canada Free Trade Agreement.
9th: The 25th ASEAN-Australia-New Zealand Economic Ministers' Consultation:
10th: The 17th ASEAN-India Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultations agreed to further strengthen economic ties through cooperation in mutually beneficial fields such as healthcare, start-ups and technology, connectivity, empowerment of women and youth, as well as skills development.
11th: The 17th ASEAN-Korea Economic Ministers' Consultation:
The consultations agreed to further strengthen the liberalization of products on the sensitive list (ST) under the ASEAN-Korea Trade Agreement after the RCEP was signed.
The consultations also welcomed the implementation of effective activities under the Korea Technology Consulting and Solutions Program (TASK) in 2020, helping to improve productivity as well as product quality and standards and provide opportunities for local industries to access. Knowledge of technology of Korean experts.
The consultations also supported and noted the progress made in establishing the ASEAN-Korea Industrial Innovation Center and the ASEAN-Korea Standards Research Center.
HE Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, led a Cambodian delegation to the ASEAN Economic Ministers' Consultation with partners.
