Kampong Sralou Villagers Concerned About Infection Due to Pig and Cattle Traders Imported from Laos Without Proper Control by Officials
(Preah Vihear): Many residents of Kampong Sralao village, Kampong Sralao commune, Chhep district, Preah Vihear province, bordering Laos, have expressed concern over the spread of infectious diseases after a group of traders imported pigs and cattle. Goats from Laos without proper inspection by professional officials.
Residents said that while Kovid 19 was spreading, they were concerned about various infectious diseases, especially animal-to-animal and human-to-human transmission. This concern is due to the fact that the import of pigs, cattle, goats imported from Laos is not properly inspected by veterinary officials, and these animals are being transported to various destinations in Laos. Province or outside the province.
Regarding the import of pigs, cattle, goats from Laos to Cambodia by ferry in Kampong Sralao village, the director of the Veterinary Production Office of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Preah Vihear Province could not be reached by phone.
At the same time, the people requested Mr. Prak Sovann, Governor of Preah Vihear Province and Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to help investigate this case, especially to eliminate the import of animals from abroad to increase the price of animals. People raise families in the district as well.
Do not want to try drugs even half a time! Nationwide drug crackdown for August 30: 24 cases cracked, 61 arrested
(Phnom Penh): The nationwide campaign against illegal drugs for August 30, 2020 showed that law enforcement forces cracked down on 24 cases, including 10 cases of use and 14 cases of trafficking, arresting 61 suspects.
According to the report, from January 1 to August 30, 2020, law enforcement forces cracked down on 7,002 cases and detained 14,003 people (880 females). Rural Development Bank and Agriculture: Despite Delays in Harvesting Fragrant Rice, Cambodia Still Ensures Food Security and Rice Exports
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kao Thach, Director General of the Bank for Development of Agriculture and Rural Development, said that although the drought has caused some impact on the people's harvest of fragrant rice, Cambodia still ensures food security and exports. Rice in 2020 according to the vision of the Royal Government.
He said that as of August, farmers have been planting rain-fed rice faster than in the same period in 2019. Harvesting light rice at the beginning of the season yields an average of 4 tons per hectare.
The river water condition this year is lower than in 2019, which until now, the water has not yet reached the paddy area, so farmers can switch from cultivating paddy to cultivating wet season rice, which can increase the growing season. Add and increase the area under rainy season rice cultivation.
As a result of this cultivation, Cambodia was able to ensure adequate food security and serve rice exports as planned, despite the early rains but later droughts that affected the area. Grow some fragrant rice.
Harvesting of light rice (fragrant and ER) at the beginning of this year is slower than in 2019 due to the drought, resulting in slow planting and severely affected rice, which requires farmers to plow and cultivate. Some re-harvest and the harvest will be extended until October, November and December, along with the harvest of jasmine-romduol rice.
Therefore, in order to achieve the target of exporting 800,000 tons of rice, the Cambodian Rice Federation has requested an additional $ 30 million in credit support under the Royal Government's special program through the Rural Development and Agriculture Bank (THBB). And apply for credit under this program for 12 months.
Agriculture Minister urges people to make more efforts to grow crops to meet domestic demand, reduce imports
(Siem Reap): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in the situation of Kovid-19 Infectious Diseases, requested the people to make more efforts on crop production, increase production volume, ensure quality and improve packaging processing capacity to compete. Meet market needs and consumer preferences, increase household income and reduce poverty, especially reduce imports.
The Minister's request was made on the occasion of inspecting the vegetable production activities of Samrong Meanchey Agricultural Community at Mr. Chheav Hong's vegetable farm in Samrong Tbong Village, Samrong Commune, Sot Nikum District, Siem Reap.
