Legal Awareness: Childhood
In order to find out more clearly, today, Fresh News has cooperated with the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation to raise the topic of "Botpheap" to explain and share to all citizens as follows:
According to the Convention on the Rights of the Child, Article 7 states that children are registered immediately after birth and have the right to have a name from birth, have the right to a nationality and, as far as possible, children have the right to Get to know your parents and get parental care.
The Convention makes it clear that newborns are born and have the right to know their parents, so it is imperative for children to have a clear identity and to become full citizens, including Warm from his parents.
For a person who is supposed to be a father, filing a paternity denial of the child must be made within six months of knowing the birth of the child, but the calculation of the period will vary if the person Assuming the father is a person under the general guardianship, this period shall be counted from the time of the cancellation of the general guardianship announcement.
As for the child, if the child is a minor, the mother or guardian for the minor must act as a representative in filing a petition denying the paternity, but if not, the court will appoint a special representative.
A paternity denial claim will not be valid if a period of six months has elapsed from the time he or she becomes an adult, but if the child is under the general guardianship period from that time onwards. Cancel General Guardian Startup Announcement. (Articles 990 and 991 of the Civil Code).
However, this claim will no longer be effective if a period of 20 (twenty) years has elapsed from the date of the child's birth or the date on which the recognition effect occurred. However, if you are an adult, you need to get your consent before you can file a complaint.
In particular, for children who were abandoned by their mothers at an early age or for some reason who did not know the mother and later learned about the identity of the woman who gave birth, the child may file a claim to confirm the existence of maternity to the woman. Even if the child dies, the child's relatives can still file a complaint, which is one year from the time of filing the mother.
The mother can also file a claim to prove the existence of maternity to the child, but if the child dies, the claim can only be made if the child has a direct relative. On the other hand, if the child or direct relative is a minor, the mother must obtain the consent of the child or direct relative before filing this complaint.
In short, paternity refers to the birth of a child by the parents, and the child has the right to register, obtain citizenship, receive care, be educated and know his or her parents.
