Islamic professors explain Islam's Asura holiday, while some people still mistake it for a cruel ritual
But in fact, the festival is a reminder of the merits of Allah, the Lord, who saved the Prophet Moses from the genocide of the Pharaohs of the ancient Egyptians.
* Professor Musa: Asura Day is the 10th day of Muharram, the first month of the Islamic calendar.
* Professor Musa: In Islam, Muslims who are able to be venerated but are not obliged to do so are voluntary, and the ritual, as stated in Islamic law, is to remove the sins of the past. A year passed.
* Professor Musa: There are three stages in this Asura fasting in the time of the last Prophet of Allah, Muhammad:
* Stage 1: At the city of Makkah, the Prophet Muhammad was venerated alone, not yet used for Muslims. * Stage 2: After fleeing to Madinah, he venerated and used Muslims to venerate. This means that it is an obligation on Muslims. * Stage 3: Later, when Allah made Ramadan an annual obligatory day, this Asura fast was changed to Sunnah or voluntary.
