Speaking at the meeting, Ms. Ros Sophany said that the Royal Government has set a policy to create human resources with quality and virtue, that is, people full of knowledge, skills, clear and good attitude to contribute to the development of the country. It will be a high middle income country by 2030 and a high income country by 2050.
She stated that in the phase of the Ministry authorizing the reopening of state public schools nationwide, the second phase, the management of each school management must be adequately prepared, such as: thermometers, soap, alcohol or Also antiseptic gel and a protective mask.
According to the instructions of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, Mr. Chhou Bunroeung, Director of the Department of Education, confirmed that the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports has allowed public public schools across the country to re-teach ninth and twelfth grade students nationwide. From September 7, 2020, which is the second phase until the end of the program until the end of November 2020, without a major vacation.
At the same time, the Ministry of Education has set the high school diploma exam period to be set for December 21, 2020. Separately, the lower secondary school diploma examination period will be set for November 30, 2020. Eleven Capital-Provincial Hospital Officers Receive Training on "Management and Leadership"
(Phnom Penh): Management officers at 11 Capital-Provincial Referral Hospitals were trained on "Management and Leadership" focusing on soft skills focusing on effective communication and leadership. Better staff and service to the people.
Dr. Tea Phalla, the facilitator of the training, said that the purpose of this training is to add software leadership to hospital leaders in addition to the technical skills of doctors.
Dr. Tea Phalla continued that this soft skill is to build good relationships, self-awareness, rationality, virtue, strong will in work, empathy and good cooperation with subordinates so that the leadership has influence that is highly valued in Providing good health services to the people, especially the poor, with equity and dignity.
In addition, "We want the director of the hospital to be a leader who is trusted by the staff and patients, because this soft skill is the foundation of success up to 85% in any field. "Business hospitals do not have good results without this skill." The five-day training course also included theories of Buddhist education, as well as mind-body training and meditation.
At the same time, Mr. Ieng Sotheara, a well-known businessman in Cambodia, also shared his experience that this training course is a new turning point where the hospital management is learning more new leadership methods outside of their specialization based on Morality, deep virtues according to Buddhist theory.
He continued, "Studying and understanding the Buddhist theory is not only in any field, but if we study in depth and apply it in daily life, we will be happy for ourselves, our families and our society." For the hospital, the more consistent the practice of truth and gratitude, the better will be the trust and hope of patients.
Not only the leadership of the hospital, the business of all sectors, if more clearly understood, those sectors will grow in a way that is harmonious, safe, cool and peaceful country forever.
Meanwhile, Dr. Chab Chanthyda, Deputy Director of Kampong Cham Provincial Hospital, who received this training, said that after studying this course, he felt calm and knew himself and his work better. She added that she will apply this knowledge and skills to her officers.
Please be informed that this training course to complement health officers on general medical skills will be established by the Ministry of Health and led by the National Institute of Public Health (NIPH) in 2017. The program is designed for sub-national health managers, including health center managers and hospital managers.
The goal is for the next generation of health facility leaders to have high management capacity and be able to exercise leadership in strengthening health services to better meet community needs and ensure equity for people. .
