General knowledge: At least 50% of the Earth's oxygen is from the oceans
The percentage of oxygen produced in the oceans is difficult to quantify because its production volumes are constantly changing. Scientists can use satellite imagery to track photosynthesis and estimate the amount of photosynthesis that occurs in the oceans, but the use of satellite imagery does not completely tell the situation.
Remember that although the oceans produce at least 50 percent of the Earth's oxygen, living things in the oceans need to use about the same amount of oxygen. Like terrestrial animals and plants, marine animals and plants need oxygen. On the other hand, when they die and rot, the oceans also need oxygen.
This area is often referred to as a "ghost zone" because oxygen levels are too low to sustain most life in the oceans. The US Center for Ocean Science has been conducting research and forecasting of the presence of algae and seawater along with Hypoxia to reduce the impact on ocean ecosystems.
