The Arctic Ocean is becoming increasingly polluted, according to Norwegian researchers.
According to research from the University of Nord in Norway, traffic across the Arctic Ocean's busiest lanes, along the Siberian coast of Russia, increased by 58% between 2016 and 2019. . Last year, a total of 2,694 ships crossed the strait.
The ship's traffic was driven primarily by Russia, China and Canada, transporting iron ore, oil, LNG and other fuels across the Arctic Ocean. Although 2020 has been plagued by global epidemics, Arctic shipping is still on the rise, with significant impacts on the shipping industry around the world.
However, the growth of Arctic vessels is a concern for the environment, as large ships use fuel to emit carbon dioxide and black matter to the surface. Arctic ice and snow. This causes the sun's radioactive material to be absorbed rather than thrown back into the atmosphere and, as a result, exacerbates the temperature rise in the area.
